\par\par \b \uc2 USD/PHP Rebounds But Chart Says Sustained Recovery Unlikely -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2015\par 04:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/PHP is likely to remain under pressure despite rebounding to 44.17 from its Monday close of 44.09. The strong chart resistance of the 200-day moving average - currently at 44.28 - may deter traders from betting on a sustained USD/PHP rally in the near term. Instead, short-term speculators may attempt to sell the dollar if it reaches the 200-day moving average line, betting on an impending downside break of the 44.00 round-figure trading barrier - which if breached may trigger stoploss orders to sell USD/PHP. The possibility of financial market distress over Greek bonds and continued oil price weakness has been detrimental to risk-taking in other parts of the world and thus firming up the safe haven U.S. dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY is extending earlier loss to fall to as low as 118.13 (from around 118.50) following Economy Minister Akira Amari's comments that the government and the BOJ haven't set a clear deadline for 2% inflation. The comments suggest "the government side is slightly loosening its interpretation" about the inflation target, thus receding expectations for early monetary easing by the BOJ and inviting the JPY buying, says a senior European bank dealer. The pair is now at 118.22. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0314 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The yuan has been rising against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of other emerging economies despite its weakness against the U.S. dollar, and this will likely put pressure on China's exporters this year, economists at Capital Economics say. The research house cited U.S. data as showing that prices of imports from China have remained stable in dollar terms while prices from other emerging economies have been falling. "In recent weeks, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems to have been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the yuan from weakening. [The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] will come under pressure to allow the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slide if exports start to suffer," its economists say in a note to clients. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0308 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/THB's bearish technical bias remains as long as the 32.60 top of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel is intact by Tuesday's close. Though the U.S. dollar is broadly supported in Asia, USD/THB has been on a controlled descent over the past two weeks, augmented by bearish technical chart signals that suggest a move to 32.34 is possible in the near term. Amid spates of risk aversion due to crude oil prices collapsing, a potentially destabilizing Greek election outcome, and escalation in the Russia-Ukraine conflict, the baht has been rising. Thailand is typically less affected by Western risk aversion compared to its peers, but it may show more reliance on Japan's export performance due to heavy investment in production facilities by Japanese companies. USD/THB is now 32.59 from its Monday close of 32.56. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY is slightly weaker at 118.31 (falling as low as 118.24 from around 118.50) in late morning as the USD buying "ran its course," says a Japanese bank dealer. The market mood turned toward risk with Tokyo stocks going up and U.S. yields increasing, inviting USD buying. "But the buying failed to last," the dealer says. Another dealer says the pair seemed show a slight but quick falloff due to Japanese Economy Minister Akira Amari's comments that the government and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  haven't set clear deadline for a 2% inflation target. Mr. Amari also said both an unlimited fall or unlimited gain in the yen would be undesirable. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PAB \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0238 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Japanese government should keep its policy of providing financing for the construction of new coal-fired power plants overseas in order to support Japanese power plants makers, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Makiko Ono. In Asia, Chinese power plant makers are expanding their businesses as the country's own demand for new coal-power plants slow. Sometimes their businesses comes with low-interest rate financing by China state-owned banks, against which Japan should compete, Ono says in a report. Many developed countries have said they wouldn't lend money for construction of new coal-fired power plants because they want to curb global warming. (mari.iwata@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0232 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan government bonds fall, tracking overnight losses in U.S. Treasurys as concerns over the Greece election results eased, says a local trader. Investors are turning cautious after recent sharp gains in local bonds after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced larger-than-expected bond-buying stimulus Thursday. The five-year bond yield has hit record low at 0.994% Monday. "The below-one-percent yield of the five-year bonds was considered really too low...so some investors prefer to take profits now," the trader adds. But the outlook for local bonds remains sound as more market participants expect the U.S. to put off its expected interest rate hike until later this year. The five-year yield rebounds to 1.0055% from 0.9985% late Monday; the 10-year yield rises to 1.4988% from 1.4848%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0227 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the Singapore dollar is increasingly overvalued given the nation's "dismal" productivity growth over the years and tips the USD/SGD to reach 1.4000 by end-2015. The pair is currently at 1.3435, a shade below Monday's close. \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimates the Singapore dollar's nominal effective exchange rate has started to trade below the midpoint of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2 's undisclosed trading band. \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  says its base case scenario is that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will ease its USD-selling behavior while keeping its monetary policy parameters unchanged. However, if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  considers easing in April, it could widen the policy band, it adds. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0227 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's resumed use of 28-day reverse repurchase agreements, a cash-injection tool, following a two-year break, suggests a clear intention to preempt a severe liquidity crunch ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  is injecting more cash into the banking system Tuesday by offering 30 billion yuan of seven-day reverse repos and 30 billion yuan of 28-day reverse repos. The last time it used the relatively long 28-day maturity was Jan. 8, 2013. The Lunar New Year falls on Feb. 19, kicking off a week-long public holiday that typically leads to a boom for tourism and food consumption. (hong.shen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0226 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index rose as high as 3,426.91 Tuesday--its highest since May 23, 2013--as strong Tokyo stocks lead rally in Asian equities (Japan's Nikkei Average was recently up 1.3% at 17697). Daily and weekly ST Index charts are positive-biased as MACD and stochastics on both charts are bullish, while five-period moving average is above the 15-period moving average and advancing. In coming days, the ST Index may reach 3464.79 (May 22, 2013 swing high). ST Index was recently up 0.7% at 3422.23. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0225 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/TWD is slightly lower, mostly reflecting the weaker USD/CNY, says a local trader. "We saw increasing selling pressure... possibly from foreign investors," the trader says. Rising local stocks are also supporting the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Taiex is up 0.2%. The trader adds that the USD/TWD is unlikely to rise above 31.300 in the near term despite the USD/KRW and USD/JPY remaining strong. The USD/TWD is at 31.270, slightly lower than 31.280 traded before the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday, which lifted it to 31.339 at the close of onshore trading session. Foreign investors have been net buyers of local stocks for six straight sessions. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0224 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Ample liquidity boosts Taiwan shares 0.3% higher to 9508.37, the seventh straight session of gains. However, heavyweight TSMC (2330.TW) is flat at NT$145.00, which a local trader says suggests that investors are turning cautious while the Taiex is approaching its recent high. Apple (AAPL) suppliers are largely higher ahead of the iPhone-maker earnings: Hon Hai (2317.TW) gains 0.1% to NT$88.90, Largan (3008.TW) is up 0.9% at NT$2,785.00. The trader adds that the Taiex remains biased upward ahead of futures' settlement Thursday, although profit-taking is set to limit gains from here. (aries.poon@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 26, 2015 22:18 ET (03:18 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150127eb1r000bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
