\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China watching volatile capital flows -  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  regulator\b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2015\par 06:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)               \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Jan 22 (Reuters) - China is closely monitoring its cross-border capital flows, the  \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Thursday, amid signs that money is leaving the world's second-largest economy as it slows.               \par \par \uc2 But China is able to manage risks from capital outflows as the country still posts a solid trade surplus, the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) said.               \par \par \uc2 "China's cross-border capital flows still face many uncertainties,", Guan Tao, head of the department of international payments at the SAFE, told a news conference.               \par \par \uc2 Still, China will continue to see a relatively big current account surplus this year, he added.               \par \par \uc2 "The pattern of having trade surpluses and capital outflows will become more normalised," he said.               \par \par \uc2 "We will strengthen monitoring and early warning on cross-border capital flows... and crack down on illegal capital flows."               \par \par \uc2 China's relatively fast growth and higher interest rates may help attract foreign investment, but investors may also be nervous about its economic, financial and fiscal risks.               \par \par \uc2 Globally, polices of major central banks could diverge as the U.S. Federal Reserve is "normalising" policy while central banks in Europe and Japan are stepping up policy easing, he said.               \par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  is widely expected to raise interest rates later this year.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese banks sold a net $46.5 billion in spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements in the fourth quarter of 2014, up from $16 billion in the third quarter, the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) said.               \par \par \uc2 For the whole of 2014, Chinese banks still bought a net $125.8 billion worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commercial banks bought the most yuan in seven years in December at around $19 billion, a Reuters calculation of data showed, suggesting some capital is leaving the world's second-largest economy as growth slows.               \par \par \uc2 Guan reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is gradually exiting from its regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy posted its slowest growth rate in 24 years in 2014 at 7.4 percent, and a further slowdown is expected this year.               \par \par \uc2 Economists expect policymakers to roll out more stimulus measures this year to prevent a sharper slowdown, including interest rate cuts and reductions in banks' reserve requirement ratios (RRR) to allow banks to lend more.               \par \par \uc2 Analysts at OCBC believe an RRR cut may be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weapon of choice to counter the impact of any more significant capital outflows.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-22T06:21:12.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150122eb1m004y1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
