\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan broadly steady on strong fixing\b0\par\par\uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2014\par 06:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Dec 17 (Reuters) - China's yuan held steady\par against the dollar on Wednesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set its\par daily midpoint at a fresh 10-month high, an encouraging sign of\par stability in the Chinese unit amid fears of fallout from a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis in Russia. \par     Safe-haven assets were in demand with yields on British,\par German and Japan sovereign debt hitting record lows while\par investors retreated from emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par    Fresh economic data this week indicate Chinese economic\par growth may be stabilizing at lower levels and raising\par expectations the yuan is unlikely to weaken sharply.\par  \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 fixed the official\par midpoint at 6.1137 per dollar, its strongest level since Feb. 19\par and 0.07 percent higher from Tuesday's fixing of 6.1182.\par     Spot yuan changed hands at 6.1937, broadly\par unchanged from Tuesday's close of 6.1903.\par     "I expect the yuan to remain steady or maintain a slightly\par appreciating bias next year as the economic growth stabilises\par and Beijing seeks to expand the internationalisation of the yuan\par project," said Tai Hui, chief markets strategist at JP Morgan\par Asset Management in Hong Kong.\par  \par  The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:   \par  Item                        Current    Previous    Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint               6.1137     6.1182      0.07%\par  Spot yuan                   6.1937     6.1903      -0.05%\pa