\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan slips despite 10-month high midpoint\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2014\par 04:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Dec 15 (Reuters) - China's yuan edged down\par against the dollar on Monday despite  the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  setting a\par 10-month high midpoint as more soft economic data reinforced\par expectations that new stimulus measures could be forthcoming.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the yuan's midpoint at 6.1152\par per dollar, up 0.05 percent from Friday's 6.1184. It was the\par strongest level since Feb 20.\par     Spot yuan changed hands at 6.1928 near midday,\par down 0.1 percent from Friday's close at 6.1869. It traded\par between 6.1855 and 6.1944 in the morning.\par     China's economy showed further signs of fatigue in November,\par with factory output growth slowing more than expected and growth\par in investment near a 13-year low, putting pressure on\par policymakers to loosen monetary conditions. \par     Analysts expected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut banks' reserve\par requirement ratios (RRR) by the end of the year, and reduce\par benchmark interest rates early next year, to shore up an economy\par set to show its weakest growth in 24 years.\par     China told banks to increase lending in the final months of\par 2014 and relaxed enforcement of loan-to-deposit ratios to expand\par credit. In a sign that banks were already responding to\par Beijing's instructions to reflate the economy, new lending\par jumped 56 percent in November.  \par     Such monetary easing measures will flood the market with\par yuan funds and put pressure on the yuan's value.     \par     The spot rate is currently allowed to trade 2 percent above\par or 