\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan firms but trades well below \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's stronger midpoint\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2014\par 07:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BEIJING, Dec 11 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against the\par dollar on Thursday but continued to trade far below the official\par daily guidance rate as markets remained bearish due to\par speculation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will loosen monetary policy\par further.\par     The spot market opened at 6.17 per dollar, up 0.11 percent\par from the previous close. The yuan was changing hands at 6.1718\par at midday, 0.92 percent below the midpoint.\par     The prospect of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  cutting the\par reserve requirement ratio (RRR) for Chinese banks, which would\par flood the market with renminbi and drive down its value, hung\par over the market.\par     Analysts have predicted that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could cut RRR\par as soon as this month in attempt to boost flagging economic\par growth, by further relaxing monetary policy after a surprise cut\par in loan and deposit rates in November.\par     The yuan had closed up 0.2 percent against the dollar on\par Wednesday as traders suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par stabilise the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after it fell 0.59 percent on Monday and\par Tuesday.\par  \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  followed up before trading opened on Thursday by\par setting a stronger midpoint rate for the yuan of\par 6.1153 per dollar, up 0.07 percent from the previous fix, and\par the strongest midpoint set since February.\par     The spot rate is currently allowed to trade 2 percent above\par or below the midpoint.\par     In the offshore market, not subject to the restrictions\par imposed onshore, the market is proving even more bearish. \par     Non-deliverable forwards traded at a nearly 3 percent\par discount to the fixing rate, indicating punters are betting on\par long-term weakness in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The offshore spot rate was 0.11 percent below the onshore\par rate.\par     \par \par The onshore spot yuan market at a glance: \par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint      6.1153   6.1195      0.07%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.1718   6.1768      0.08%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.92%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change ytd                       -1.91%\par  Spot change since 2005                34.10%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par     \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:\par  Instrument            Current  Difference\par                                 from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.1785     -0.11%\par           *                     \par  Offshore              6.3015     -2.95%\par  non-deliverable                \par  forwards                       \par                **               \par  \par \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \