\par\par \b \uc2 IFR Markets ForexWatch Asia Regional Daily Briefing\b0\par\par\uc2 3,018 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2014\par 00:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SYDNEY, Dec 10 (IFR) - \par     \par News and data out of Europe on Tuesday\par * GB Nov BRC Retail sales 0.9% vs prev 0.0%. 0.8% exp\par * CH Nov Jobless u/adj 3.2% vs prev 3.1%. Adj 3.1% vs 3.1% rvsd. 3.2% exp\par * DE Oct Exports SA -0.5% vs prev 5.50%. -1.5% exp\par * DE Oct Imports SA -3.1% vs prev 5.2% rvsd. -1.5% exp\par * DE Oct Trade balance SA 20.6bln vs prev 18.6bln rvsd. 18.5bln exp \par * GB Oct Ind ouput -0.1% m/m, +1.1% y/y vs prev 0.7%/0.8% rvsd. 0.2%/1.8% exp\par * GB Oct Mfg output -0.7% m/m, +1.7% y/y vs prev 0.6% rvsd/2.9%. 0.2%/3.2% exp\par  \par News and data out of North America\par * Fitch places Japan's 'A+' long-term local & Fgn CCY IDRS on rating watch\par negative\par * ECB's Praet asked if ECB will make decision on QE in January says could make\par decision and implement later, says ECB acts independently does not take pending\par judicial review of OMT into account\par * ECB's Praet if we had room to cut rates last week think would have done so,\par cannot deny there are signals of concern in inflation expectations\par * ECB's Makuch when expectations change in negative way the probability of\par non-standard measures grow\par * Germany's Schaeuble German govt to introduce motion in parliament to extend\par bailout for Greece\par * NZ's Fonterra cuts '14/'15 Farmgate milk price to NZD 4.70/kgms from NZD 5.30,\par global milk supply remains greater than demand\par * ITAU BBA CEO says scaling down of BNDES loans must be done gradually, local\par markets should pick up slack\par * Brazil's Tombini repeats CB will not be complacent w/inflation, working to\par bring inflation back to center of 4.5% target, must let \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adjust to\par market conditions\par * US  NFIB Business Optimism Idx   Nov     98.1, 96.1-prev\par * US  ICSC Chain Stores WW   w/e      -1.5%, -1.8%-prev\par * US  ICSC Chain Stores YY   w/e      2.9%, 2.8%-prev\par * US  Redbook MM w/e      -1.3%, -0.6%-prev\par * US  Redbook YY  w/e   3.9%, 4.8%-prev\par * US  JOLTS Job Openings  Oct       +4.834m, f/c 4.790m, +4.685m-prev\par * US  IBD Economic Optimism   Dec      48.4, 46.4-prev\par * US  Wholesale Inventories MM  Oct       0.4%, f/c 0.2%, 0.4%-prev\par * US  Wholesale Sales MM Oct       0.2%, f/c 0.1%, 0%-prev\par * MX  Headline Inflation  Nov     0.81%, f/c 0.82%, 0.55%-prev\par * MX  Core Inflation  Nov     0.16%, f/c 0.16%, 0.17%-prev\par * MX  12-Month Inflation  Nov     4.17%, f/c 4.18%, 4.3%-prev\par * BAML S&P 500 at 2200 by YE '15, US GDP growth at 3.3% in '15, US 10-yr to\par 2.75% by end of '15\par  \par IFR Morning Brief for Wednesday Dec 10 (Early-Bird Edition)\par  \par Themes\par  \par * The main themes across asset markets on Tuesday was a rise in investor risk\par aversion and a spike in volatility.\par * The initial catalyst for the increased risk aversion was a 5.31% fall in the\par Shanghai Composite following moves by China to tighten the collateral\par requirements for short-term financing\par * European stock markets fell sharply in part due to the poor lead from Asia -\par but in great part to another bout of political uncertainty in Greece after the\par Greek government unexpectedly brought forward the presidential vote. The Greek\par stock market collapsed 12.4% in response to the surprise announcement.\par * The German DAX fell 2.21%; the French CAC closed down 2.55%; the FT Milan\par Index had a 2.81% slide and the Spanish IBEX was crushed 3.18% lower.\par * The VIX index traded up to 16.68 at one stage - 17.4% higher than Monday's\par 14.21 close and 44.5% higher than Friday's post US NFP low at 11.53. The VIX\par index eased from the highs when Wall Street came off the lows and was trading at\par 15.31 late in the US session - up around 8% for the day.\par * Wall Street was down 1.25% at one stage when the banks sold off in response to\par comments from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  executives indicating Q4 profits\par will be negatively impacted by regulatory fines in the case of Citi and trading\par revenue in the case of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par * The KBW Bank Index was down 1.58% at one stage before recovering in the\par afternoon to be down less than 0.15%.\par * The recovery in the banks helped the \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  to pare back the losses and late in\par the US session it was down 0.33% after being down 1.26% earlier.\par * The spike in risk aversion had a big impact on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, as the initial\par response was to pare back existing positions - and the overwhelming position in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  world leading into this week was long US dollars to the eye-balls.\par * The combination of the rise in risk aversion and unwinding of USD longs sent\par the USD/JPY into a free-fall with stop loss sell orders triggered all the way\par down to 117.90 - down 2.30% from the Monday close and 3.25% from the Monday\par 121.86 high.\par * Once the recent long USD/JPY positions were obliterated - the USD/JPY then\par staged a vicious reversal higher when Wall Street recovered from early losses to\par be down less than 0.50%. The USD/JPY was back above 119.40 late in the US\par session - down a little over 1.0% from Monday's close.\par * The risk aversion environment pushed long-dated \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields lower and\par this also weighed on the USD/JPY and USD overall.\par * The 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield traded down to 2.18% at one stage before\par recovering to 2.22% late in the US session - down 3 BPS from Monday's close at\par 2.25% and 12 BPS down from the post US NFP high at 2.34%.\par * The rise in risk aversion combined with USD selling gave gold strong support -\par and it traded up to 1.238 at one stage before settling around 1,230 late in the\par US session - up 2.33% for the day.\par * The steep fall in the oil price on Monday was the main catalyst for the rise\par in risk aversion and volatility on Monday - but both crude oil and other key\par commodities were well behaved on Tuesday.\par * Brent Crude was around 1.0% higher late in the US session while NYMEX Crude\par was up over 1.15%. NY Copper followed the strong lead from gold and was up close\par to 1.20% late in the US session while iron ore was fixed 30 cents lower at\par 69.40.\par * The strong performance in commodities helped to underpin the commodity\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  despite the spike higher in risk aversion and the heavy buying of JPY\par on the crosses.\par * The AUD/USD traded as high as 0.8371 when the US dollar was sold across the\par board - as funds wound back their outstanding USD longs. The bounce back in the\par USD/JPY stemmed the broad USD selling and the AUD/USD was trading at 0.8309 late\par in the US session - up 0.17% on the day.\par * The AUD was the underperformer in the broad USD selloff, as the NZD was one of\par the best performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for most of Tuesday. The NZD/USD traded as high\par as 0.7762 when the USD was getting clobbered - up 1.42% at one stage.\par *  The NZD/USD retreated to 0.7710 and steadied around that level. It then sold\par off to 0.7686 when Fonterra cut the forecast on the milk price - and was trading\par around 0.7695 late in the US session - up 0.55% on the day.\par * Despite the renewed uncertainty in Greece and large selloffs in Euro zone\par equity markets - the EUR/US surged higher, as investor position squaring trumped\par fundamentals.\par * The EUR/USD traded as high as 1.2448 at one stage - up 1.05% from Monday's\par 1.2317 close. The EUR/USD fell back below 1.2400 during the US afternoon session\par when the USD/JPY bounced back. Late in the US session the EUR/USD was trading at\par 1.2376 - up 0.48% from Monday's close.\par * The Euro zone bond market didn't have a particularly large reaction to the\par renewed uncertainty in Greece. The 10-year Greek bond yield surged from 7.34% to\par 8.13% - but that is still below where it was trading around the end of November.\par * The spread between other peripheral EZ debt and German Bunds widened a bit -\par but not significantly. The 10-year Spanish bond yield rose 3 BPS to 1.82%; the\par 10-year Italian bond yield rose 7 BPS to 2.02% while the 10-year German Bund\par yield eased 2 BPS to 0.69%.\par * The US dollar index was trading at 88.71 late in the US session - down 0.37%\par from Monday's close - but up 0.64% from the intraday low at 88.15.\par  \par Wrap-up \par     \par What a difference a couple of days make in the wonderful world of markets. The\par investor landscape looked clear and safe following the unambiguously strong US\par non-farm payroll data on Friday. Risk assets looked as safe as houses,\par volatility was at multi-month lows and even beaten down commodities started to\par look appealing if the US economy was going to bounce back hard in 2015, as the\par job numbers suggested. Probably the surest bet emerging from the US payroll data\par was that US yields would climb along with the US dollar - as the Fed would most\par definitely start prepping the markets for the commencement of the tightening\par cycle around mid-2015.\par  \par In the past two trading sessions has seen equity markets selloff, volatility\par surge, oil prices and key commodities collapse and recover; long dated Treasury\par yields fall and the USD get hammered hard. It appears that investors are looking\par for excuses to pare back ahead of year-end, as the catalysts for the sharp rise\par in risk aversion and volatility seem to be a bit flimsy.\par  \par Despite the shake-out in the USD/JPY - it is still trending higher and looks set\par to close above the key tankan line at 119.55. The last couple of days has been a\par reminder that volatility can show up at anytime and there is no such thing as a\par one-way bet - but the fundamental and technical backdrop still supports the view\par the USD/JPY will likely test levels above 124.00 in the coming weeks.\par  \par The AUD/USD continues to be a "sell the rally" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as far as hedge funds\par are concerned. The AUD might get some support from AUD/NZD buying as the\par Fonterra outlook for milk prices is loaded with uncertainty and likely to weigh\par on NZD sentiment ahead of the RBNZ decision tomorrow.\par  \par China CPI is out today and will be the main event for the Asian session. The\par market is looking for 1.6% Y/Y and a weaker reading could stir up global\par growth/disinflation concerns and weigh on risk assets and risk \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \par John.Noonan@thomsonreuters.com\par   \par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Outlook\par  \par USD/Asians followed the USD/JPY's roller coaster ride overnight. Massive risk\par selling on China's stock markets free fall, renewed Greek political worries sent\par the USD tumbling as traders sold risk. USD/SGD collapsed below 1.3160 support,\par pair skidded to 1.3136 before recovering to 1.3163 into the close. MYR implieds\par held steady above 3.4800 despite the sell-off as oil/export woes dominate\par sentiment. Oil prices recovered slightly overnight but bearish outlook intact.\par Focus in Asia today remains on China  - Nov CPI and PPI eyed. CPI exp to rise\par 1.6%y/y, unchanged from previous; PPI f/c at -2.4%y/y vs 2.2%y/y previous. PBOC\par likely to continue to fix the USD/CNY lower but should not have any impact on\par trades. Markets still short the pair - wary of further short squeeze. Thai\par markets closed for holiday today.\par  \par USD/KRW implieds reeled overnight, heavy sell stops tripped on break below 1110.\par Pair likely to see further choppy trades amid 1103-1108 intraday. Heavy inflows\par into stocks, JPY/KRW selling interests to continue to weigh on pair. Expect BOK\par to stall though. S Korea Nov import prices -2.1%y/y vs -4.1% in Oct; export\par prices -2.1%y/y vs -4.1% in Oct. Increased calls for BOK to cut rates tmr but\par overall consensus see no change, exp BOK to leave base rate at 2.0%. KRW NDFs\par traded 1103.5-1111.5 rg o/n, clsd at 1105.5-1106.5 in NY.\par  \par USD/SGD whacked lower overnight, follows broad sell-off in G7 pairs. Stops hit\par on break below 1.3160. Pair nosedived to 1.3136 low but bounced back to close\par 1.3163 in NY. Underlying bullish bias intact, as growth/exports slowdown, China\par uncertainty underpins. May see range amid 1.3150-1.3200 intraday - eyes JPY, CNY\par leads.\par  \par USD/MYR implieds slipped to 3.4750 low overnight but stalled on fresh bidding\par interests. Depressed oil prices, despite calm overnight, continues to weigh on\par pair. Jitters in China markets, sell-off in MGS, outflows to continue to\par pressure the MYR. Expect the USD/MYR to remain well bid on dips below 3.4800\par intraday. Break above 3.50, targeting 3.55-3.60 remains on cards. NDFs traded\par 3.4850-3.4950 range overnight, closed at 3.4935-85 in NY.\par  \par USD/THB tanked on heavy selling, markets still like to short pair. Steady rule\par from the military govt, THB/JPY plays weigh on USD/THB. Weak longs scuttled on\par break below 32.95 overnight. Pair slumped to 32.83 low, closed 32.84 in NY. Thai\par markets closed for local holiday today. Should see thin interests amid\par 32.85-32.90 range.\par  \par USD/IDR likely to continue firm bias around the 12350 level today, exp\par 12340-12380 range. NDFs remain well bid above 12400 overnight, despite increased\par risk aversion. All eyes on mass protests in Jakarta today. NDFs traded\par 12400-12460 range overnight, closed at 12445-12465 in NY.\par  \par USD/PHP may range 44.50-44.70 intraday.  Strong OFW inflows to continue to cap\par rallies but China worries, bullish USD theme to underpin. PHP NDFs  little\par changed overnight, traded 44.60-70 in NY, clsd at 44.66-71. Oct exports data\par eyed, last 15.70%y/y.\par  \par USD/TWD likely to trade 31.15-32.30 range in Far East. Buy dips remain preferred\par strategy. Exporters sales to cap rallies but cont'd \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  worries underpin. NDFs traded 31.10-31.20 range o/n, closed at 31.11-31.13\par in NY.\par  \par USD/CNY trades to remain choppy today. Plunge in stock markets, liquidity prob\par on govt's new bond rules to keep markets on toes. Expect the repo and bond yield\par curves to continue higher. USD/CNY ended at 6.1855-6.1900 yest, after range amid\par 6.1706-6.2059 intraday. USD/CNH held bullish bias overnight too, last at\par 6.1860-90. Pair traded 6.1713-6.2053 range yesterday. NDF curve stayed firm,\par despite some selling interests overnight. 1mth last at 6.1380-00, traded\par 6.1380-6.1430 range o/n. 6mths ind at 6.2350-60, off 6.2400 high yesterday. 1yr\par last at 6.3140-70, off overnight high at 6.3220. Nov CPI and PPI eyed. CPI exp\par to rise 1.6%y/y, unchanged from previous; PPI f/c at -2.4%y/y vs 2.2%y/y\par previous.\par  \par USD/INR well bid overnight on short cover, stocks fall. NDFs traded 62.18-62.33\par range in NY, closed at 62.30-62.35. Sensex ended -1.15% yesterday, investors\par booked profit amid global stocks sell-off, risk aversion. USD/INR closed at\par 61.89 onshore, after trading amid 61.8475-61.9175 intraday. Pair likely to open\par bid above 62.00, exp 62.00-62.20 range intraday.\par Catherine.tan@thomsonreuters.com\par \par OVERNIGHT RANGES----------------------------INTRADAY RANGES - Close 21:00GMT\par NDFS OPEN   HIGH    LOW     LAST       VOL  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2    HIGH    LOW     CLOSE\par IDR  12420  12460   12400   12445-65   Hi   USD/JPY   121.01  117.90  119.61\par INR  62.19  62.33   62.18   62.30-35   Hi   EUR/USD   1.2448  1.2292  1.2371\par KRW  1110   1111.5  1103.5  1105.5-6.5 Hi   EUR/JPY   148.85  146.80  147.96\par MYR  3.4850 3.4950  3.4850  3.4935-85  Hi   GBP/USD   1.5720  1.5626  1.5666\par PHP  44.70  44.70   44.63   44.66-71   Hi   USD/CAD   1.1501  1.1398  1.1439\par TWD  31.13  31.20   31.10   31.11-13   Hi   AUD/USD   0.8371  0.8223  0.8297\par CNY  1-mth  6.1430  6.1380  6.1380-00       NZD/USD   0.7762  0.7609  0.7677\par CNY  6-mth  6.2400  6.2380  6.2350-60       USD/SGD   1.3226  1.3136  1.3163\par CNY  1-yr   6.3220  6.3180  6.3140-70       USD/THB    33.05   32.83   32.84\par  \par Equities  Close   Change  %Change  UST(Yields)    Close    Previous\par DJIA      17801      -51    -0.29  10-year        2.21%      2.26%\par S&P 500    2060    -0.49    -0.02   2-year         0.61%      0.63%\par Nasdaq     4766      +26    +0.54  30-year        2.86%      2.90%\par FTSE       6529     -143    -2.14  Spot Gold($) 1230.00    1203.50\par DAX        9793     -221    -2.21  Nymex          63.88      62.99\par Nikkei    17813     -122    -0.68  Brent          66.62      66.19\par        \par \par  (Reporting by John Noonan, Catherin Tan and Peter Whitley)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-12-12T23:25:01.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141209eac900ted\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
