\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan slips, likely to have a second weekly loss\b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2014\par 04:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Dec 5 (Reuters) - China's yuan edged up slightly\par against the dollar on Friday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a\par stronger midpoint, though it appeared headed for a second\par straight weekly loss. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 fixed the official\par midpoint at 6.1373 per dollar, up 0.06 percent from Thursday's\par 6.1411.\par     Spot yuan changed hands at 6.1524 near midday, up\par 0.04 percent from the previous day's close at 6.1546. It has\par depreciated 0.12 percent for the week.\par     "Demand to buy dollars is strong this week, especially from\par oil and coal companies," said a trader at a Chinese bank in\par Shanghai.\par     The yuan remained touched a 3-1/2 month low on Thursday,\par following a strong rebound of global dollar index.\par     It is expected to fall further next year when the U.S.\par starts to increase interest rates while China's need for exports\par to counterbalance the property-led downturn at home makes\par further appreciation less desirable, analysts say.    \par     "We see the CNY weakening slightly against a stronger USD,\par trading at 6.35 by end 2015 and 6.40 by end 2016, with periodic\par periods of appreciation and increased two-way volatility," \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par analysts said in a Nov. 26 report.\par     On Nov. 25, the yuan traded weaker than the official\par guidance rate for the first time since late September, as\par appetite for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  waned after a surprise rate cut four\par days earlier. \par     The spot rate is currently allowed to trade 2 percent above\par or below the midpoint.      \par \par The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:    \par  Item                       Current    Previous     Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint              6.1373     6.1411       0.06%\par  Spot yuan                  6.1524     6.1546       0.04%\par  Divergence from midpoint*  0.25%                   \par  Spot change ytd                                    -1.60%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation                 34.52%\par  *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par     \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:            \par  Instrument                     Current   Difference from\par                                           onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan          *  6.1524    0.00%\par  Offshore non-deliverable       6.267     -2.07%\par  forwards               **                \par  *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par     \par     >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>> \par \par     FACTBOX\par     - Yuan and capital account reforms and pilot programmes     \par  \par     \par     RECENT DEVELOPMENTS\par     - \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  cuts back on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but not totally hands\par off \par     - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  disappoints carry traders\par  \par     - Hong Kong should cherish its standing as offshore yuan hub\par - China 