\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan drops, set for 1st monthly decline since April\b0\par\par\uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2014\par 06:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Nov 28 (Reuters) - China's yuan fell on Friday as\par companies' demand for dollars surged at month-end, with the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on course for its first monthly decline in seven\par months.\par     By midday, spot yuan was at 6.1479 per dollar,\par its lowest level in two months, and 0.14 percent weaker than\par Thursday's close of 6.1392.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 fixed the yuan's daily\par midpoint at 6.1345 per dollar, down 0.04 percent from\par previous fix. \par     "The dollar purchase outweighs sales today, partly due to\par seasonal high demand from companies, especially from oil\par companies," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai. \par     Chinese oil firms - mainly \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroChina\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sinopec Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2   - are primary dollar\par buyers on the domestic market given that the oil trade is\par conducted mostly in dollars.\par     "But the pessimistic sentiment towards the yuan after the\par interest rate cut also makes investors tend to keep more dollars\par on hand," he added, referring to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surprising\par easing late last Friday to support the cooling economy. \par     For the week, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  looked set for its biggest weekly\par loss since the week of April 21.\par     On a monthly basis, the yuan is set to drop 0.6 percent,\par ending a six-month rally since May this year, during which the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciated around 2 percent.  \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , however, has set a series of relatively\par strong midpoint fixings this week, a likely signal that it wants\par to prevent the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from a sharp depreciation.\par     Traders expect the yuan will hold steady around 6.11-6.15\par level until the end of this year. \par        \par     The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:      \par  Item                          Current    Previous    Change\par                                                       (pct)\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint                 6.1345     6.132       -0.04%\par  Spot yuan                     6.1479     6.1392      -0.14%\par  Divergence from midpoint*     0.19%                  \par  Spot change ytd                                      -1.50%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation                   34.66%\par  * Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from the official midpoint rate it sets\par each morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET\par     For the weekly CNH Tracker column, see: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/ced53w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/ced53w}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:  \par  Instrument                           Current      Difference\par                                                    from\par                                                    onshore\par                                                    (pct)\par  Offshore spot yuan          (pct)    6.1553       -0.16%\par  Offshore non-deliverable forwards    6.253        -1.90%\par                                                    \par  * Premium