\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Shares soar as China cut rates, Draghi drives for QE\b0\par\par\uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2014\par 14:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Shares soars as China cuts rates, Draghi talks bond buying\par \par \uc2 * MSCI All-World index heads for second week of gains\par \par \uc2 * Euro tumbles on Draghi, Aussie rises on China\par \par \uc2 * Yen rebounds after Japan finmin frets about pace of fall\par \par \uc2 * U.S. crude oil surges for second day after China moves\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 21 (Reuters) - World shares surged on Friday as China surprised markets with its first interest rate cut in more than two years and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  threw the door wide open to full scale money printing.\par \par \uc2 European shares, oil and other growth sensitive commodities all leapt as China's move to cut rates to 5.6 percent gave markets a welcome lift after a week where data has shown its giant economy heading for its worst year in almost quarter of a century.\par \par \uc2 It came as ECB head Draghi spoke in Frankfurt of his determination to use more aggressive measures such as large scale asset purchases -longhand for money printing- to ensure the euro zone did not slump into a new crisis.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to meet our responsibility - we will do what we must to raise inflation and inflation expectations as fast as possible," Draghi said in a heavyweight speech.\par \par \uc2 "If on its current trajectory our policy is not effective enough to achieve this ... we would step up the pressure and broaden even more the channels through which we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Both the euro zone and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been lagging the momentum of the United States, stimulus-driven Japan and faster-growing Britain over the last month, but a ramping up of the ECB's rhetoric and Beijing's actions will stoke hopes of a turnaround.\par \par \uc2 Germany's \uc2 DAX\uc2 , France's CAC and pan-regional Euro STOXX 50 were all up between 0.8 and 1 percent by 1230 GMT, leaving them on course for weekly gains of 4.5 percent, 2 percent and 2.4 percent respectively.\par \par \uc2 "The two together, (China cut, Draghi speech) suggest to me there is still a lot of hard policy work to be done next year," said Neil Williams, chief economist at fund manager Hermes in London.\par \par \uc2 Beijing's move also carried a hint of an escalating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tussle in Asia.\par \par \uc2 A sharp fall in the yen this year as Tokyo has introduced wave after wave of stimulus, has been putting the squeeze on China's exporters due to a loss of cost advantage.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso said on Friday that the yen's fall over the past week had been "too rapid". It was one of the strongest warnings against a weak yen since the aggressive stimulus efforts began two years ago and saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  leap off a 7-year low to 117.98\par \par \uc2 OIL SURGE\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets everywhere were shaken into life by China's move and the signals coming out of Frankfurt.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell sharply, slicing its way back down through $1.25 to $1.2430, while 10-year Italian government bond yields , which have been one of the biggest beneficiaries since Draghi took charge of the ECB in 2011, hit a new all-time low.\par \par \uc2 Hopes that China's growth will now quicken provided a shot in the arm for the Australian dollar <AUD=D4,> often used as a more liquid proxy for Chinese investments, and likewise lifted other key commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rate cut also added to a positive mood among oil traders, many of whom expect the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  to trim production, at what looks to be a landmark meeting in Vienna on Nov. 27.\par \par \uc2 Oil jumped 2 percent, or $1.75 to $81.07 a barrel as it surged towards its first weekly rise since mid-September in its biggest daily rise in a month.\par \par \uc2 ALL RISE\par \par \uc2 Global investor sentiment was also underpinned by record finishes by the \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  industrial average and S&P 500 on Thursday after a spate of upbeat U.S. data that offset the recent signs of spreading weakness in China and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street was expected to add a further 0.6 percent when trading resumes with the day's upbeat sentiment expected to more than make up for a lack major data.\par \par \uc2 In emerging markets, Russia's rouble <RUB=, which is closely tied to the fortunes of oil, was heading for its first weekly rise since early September as the pressure it has been under eased.\par \par \uc2 Copper and gold also got a lift, with the red metal up 1 percent and spot gold climbing to $1,197 an ounce as traders cheered the prospect of more global stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "Commodity prices have risen across the board," said \uc2 Carsten Fritsch\uc2 , senior oil and commodities analyst at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . There is hope that this step (lower Chinese interest rates) will lift commodities demand." (Additional reporting by Shanghai newsroom; Editing by Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-21T14:00:12.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141121eabl009hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
