\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan inches lower on corporate dollar demand, little changed on week\b0\par\par\uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2014\par 06:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Nov 14 (Reuters) - China's yuan edged lower\par against the dollar on Friday, with corporate dollar demand from\par oil companies helping to offset a stronger midpoint fix by the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said. \par     On Friday, spot yuan stood at 6.1255 per dollar\par at midday, 0.01 percent weaker than Thursday's close but little\par changed for the week.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  fixed the daily midpoint fixing\par  at 6.1399 per dollar, 0.03 percent firmer than\par previous fix.    \par     Chinese oil firms - mainly \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroChina\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sinopec Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2   - are primary dollar\par buyers on the domestic market given that the oil trade is still\par conducted mostly in dollars. \par     The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , however, has seen a steady rally since\par May this year, rising more than 1.9 percent against the dollar\par thanks to China's strong trade surpluses and bucking a trend of\par weakening Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid a strengthening dollar in global\par markets.\par     The dollar index has jumped 8 percent so far in the\par second half of this year.\par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is under appreciation pressure," said a trader\par at a European bank in Shanghai, adding that a stronger yuan is\par also instrumental to Chinese government's efforts to\par internationalise the yuan, such as the Shanghai-Hong Kong stock\par exchange link which debuts on Monday. \par     Most traders forecast the yuan would likely to rise to 6.10\par per dollar before the end of this year.\par     \par     The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:        \par  Item                     Current     Previous    Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint            6.1399      6.1418      0.03%\par                                                   \par  Spot yuan                6.1255      6.1248      -0.01%\par  Divergence from          -0.23%                  \par  midpoint*                                        \par  Spot change ytd                                  -1.17%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation               35.2%\par  *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET\par     For the weekly CNH Tracker column, see: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/ryd43w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/ryd43w}}}\uc2 \f2 \par    \par     \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:    \par  Instrument                 Current     Difference from\par                                         onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan         6.1291      -0.06%\par           *                             \par  Offshore non-deliverable   6.2650      -2.00%\par  forwards               **              \par  *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par .     \par     >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>> \par \par     FACTBOX\par     - Yuan and capital account reforms and pilot programmes     \par  \par     \par     RECENT DEVELOPMENTS\par     - \f28 \uc2 \f2 P