\par\par \b \uc2 China Govt Bond Traders Ignore Upbeat Chatter and Keep Selling -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2014\par 09:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China government bonds continue to extend losses despite market talk that the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  will likely inject liquidity into some small banks. The seven-year government bond yield is up at 3.53% versus 3.50% late Wednesday. "The bonds have accumulated too much gain lately. It's easy for them to fall and difficult to rise now," says a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  neither injected nor drained funds from the banking system this week, leaving the country's money market untouched for the fifth week in a row. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/TWD is slightly higher on thin volume, reflecting the U.S. dollar's strength against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say two local traders. The pair closes at 30.752--the highest onshore closing level in almost four years, versus 30.750 late Wednesday. Traders say the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have bought the U.S. dollar at around 30.615 (versus 30.610 Wednesday) to keep the pair in its target band. Most of the session's trades were done between 30.610 and 30.625. "The USD/TWD was stuck in a narrow range...buying was not active while selling became hesitant when the pair fell below 30.610," a trader says. The second trader says the USD/TWD will not likely advance much unless the USD/JPY rises to 116. Foreign fund inflows amid rising local stocks also likely support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0833 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Spain conducts a special, non-scheduled auction for its November 2024-dated inflation-linked bond, likely the last Spanish linker tap this year. Citi analysts say the auction is likely to be met with specialist demand. They add that the linker's breakeven seems to be rich (expensive in market language). The auction doesn't have a pre-set target volume, which allows the Spanish Treasury to decide flexibly about the amount sold, based on the incoming bids. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0832 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities rise in the afternoon session, climbing for a fourth straight day, as Chinese economic data miss estimates and investors bet on stimulus measures to counteract falling growth. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index climbs 0.3% to 24,019.94, hitting a two-month high, while H-shares outperform; the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index gains 0.7% to 10,800.91. That's despite a surprise deceleration in industrial output in October, rising at 7.7% on-year compared to an increase of 8% the month earlier. Fixed-asset investment and retail sales also rose by slightly less during the month than the market had anticipated. "Downward pressures on the industrial sector persisted," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Signs of stabilization in the property market notwithstanding, we still see downside pressures to growth, as a large part of the industrial sector remains under pressure." The bank adds it expects to see more monetary and fiscal easing in the months ahead. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0826 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The U.S. dollar's sustained rise in recent weeks is indication that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has several drivers that collectively are powerful, but individually are flimsy, says Ashmore head of research Jan Dehn. Dehn argues that there are several factors affecting the dollar, among these are expectations of stronger U.S. growth, a rate hike at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 , dollar purchases by Japan's Government Pension Investment Fund and hopes of quantitative easing in Europe. "U.S. dollar longs get ever-more pregnant. This is a negative technical that will eventually unwind, but probably not until everyone is up to the limit on U.S. dollar exposure," he says. (jake.watts@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0824 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Sterling remains under pressure from Wednesday's dovish \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  Inflation Report, printing a fresh 14-month low of $1.5762 in Asia. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  technical analyst \f28 \uc2 \f2 Karen Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the next major support area is between 1.5751-1.5721, which she regards as a break-point to 1.5320, which is a 78.6& Fibonacci retracement of the entire upmove seen since 2013. Now at $1.5775. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0816 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--There is an Italian government bond auction due Thursday, and Wednesday's underperformance of Italian paper versus Spanish and also versus core paper offers some relative value, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  CIB's rates strategist Orlando Green. Italy's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  offers EUR4.5 billion to EUR6 billion in January 2018, December 2021 and March 2030 BTPs. Mr. Green says that the cheapest bond in micro-relative value terms appears to be the January 2018 BTP, located in the segment that has had "very heavy" supply over the past year, so it's particularly attractive in one-year roll down terms. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0813 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's electricity output in 2014 is on track to grow at its slowest pace since 2012, a sign that last year's recovery in power production may prove short-lived. The country's power output grew 4.7% in 2012 but the growth rate later recovered to 7.6% in 2013. However, in the first 10 months of this year, China's power output grew just 4.2%, yet another sign that China still faces economic headwinds despite measures over the past two years to help jump-start the domestic economy. (wayne.ma@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0810 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Turkish markets seem to have uncoupled from most of their peers on the premise that still-falling oil prices are good for the Turkish current account balance and so likely to increase demand for the lira. Despite this, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stuck to its guns and kept the system slightly low on liquidity. However, says Garanti, if flows strengthen and the lira gains, the authorities won't hesitate to turn on the taps in a bid to lower interest rates. Garanti isn't expecting huge lira gains from here though. USD/TRY now at 2.2514. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0809 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--USD/JPY headed back toward 116.00 in Asia Thursday as talk of a snap Japanese election and postponement of next year's tax hike refused to go away. Sterling continued the slide which started with the BoE's dovish Inflation Report commentary Wednesday, falling to a fresh 14-month low of $1.5763. AUD/USD took a hit on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comments from RBA Assistant Governor Kent. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  retail sales and industrial production just missed estimates, but had little impact. Up ahead, U.S. weekly jobless claims is the highlight, already out German CPI as expected at +0.8% on the year, French CPI just a tad above expectations +0.5%. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0804 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong's third-quarter gross domestic product likely rose 1.9% on year, slightly better than second quarter's 1.8% growth, according to the median forecast of six economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. On a quarter-on-quarter basis, third-quarter GDP likely grew 0.7%, reversing the second quarter's 0.1% contraction. Economists say a better export performance has buoyed third-quarter GDP. Still, the China economic slowdown and the ongoing pro-democracy Occupy protests should hurt local consumption, weighing on the city's economy in the last quarter, says DBS economist Lily Lo. The government is scheduled to release the third-quarter GDP data at 0830 GMT on Friday. (chester.yung@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0803 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thai Finance Ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  reports that rice subsidies managed by previous governments of Yingluck and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thaksin Shinawatra\f28 \uc2 \f2  have caused paper losses of 682 billion baht or almost $21 billion. Thaksin's and Yingluck's governments spent a combined 1.1 trillion baht--or about 10% of the country's gross domestic product--to purchase rice from local growers at above-market rates. There should be about 19.2 million tons of rice in the government stockpiles, but the ministry's rice audit committee says it will cross check with another set of data to conclude the actual amount. "If the rice quality degradation [and] the missing stocks are worse than we thought, and if the price of rice sold is lower than the benchmark rate we used, then the loss figure is going to increase," says Rungson Sriworasat, the ministry's permanent secretary. Rungson says the loss figure isn't beyond expectations as the subsidies had some loopholes that opened the way for irregularities. (warangkana.chomchuen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 13, 2014 03:47 ET (08:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141113eabd001d0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
