\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan set to rise for 5th wk as market grows wary of more gains\b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2014\par 03:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Nov 7 (Reuters) - China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  edged higher\par on Friday and is set to rack up a fifth consecutive week of\par gains though a slowing economy is feeding a growing view that\par the yuan may be nearing a peak.\par     Beijing's tolerance for a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also likely to\par increase in the near term particularly as key trading partners\par such as Japan and the euro zone commit to aggressive monetary\par easings to revive their economies, sending the yen and the euro\par skidding.  \par     On Friday, spot yuan opened at 6.1165 per dollar\par and rose to 6.1146. It has gained 0.70 percent against the U.S.\par dollar over the past five weeks at a time when the greenback has\par booked broad gains against most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Spot yuan has gained even as the daily fixing has\par consistently been on the weaker side in line with the broader\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  complex. On Friday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par fixed the daily midpoint fixing at 6.1602 per dollar\par compared to 6.1565 on Thursday.    \par     "Policymakers may be growing more tolerant to a weaker yuan\par and the market is very cautious of adding aggressive long yuan\par positions," said a trader at a foreign bank in Hong Kong.    \par     While the yuan's moves against the U.S. dollar in the past\par few weeks looks tiny, it has made chunky gains on the Japanese\par yen and is breaking through key technical levels. \par     At current levels of 18.84, CNY/JPY is pushing\par past a major resistance line which marks 61.8 percent of the\par 1992-1995 drop from 24.54 to 9.57. \par     A weaker yuan is also another way to loosen monetary\par conditions quietly without cutting interest rates at a time when\par Beijing is trying to wean the economy off credit-fuelled growth.\par     In recent months, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has made use of special lending\par facilities, cut reserve requirements for major commercial banks\par and urged banks to lend at preferential rates to ensure the\par economic slowdown doesn't worsen.\par     On Thursday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pledged to maintain modest\par policy support to help the world's second-largest economy\par weather increasing headwinds in the near term but stressed that\par it will not flood markets with cash. \par     A weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may also stem the tide of hot money\par yield-seeking inflows which have resurfaced in recent trade\par data.   \par     \par     The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:        \par  Item                     Current     Previous    Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint            6.1602      6.1565      -0.06%\par                                                   \par  Spot yuan                6.1143      6.1123      -0.03%\par  Divergence from          -0.75%                  \par  midpoint*                                        \par  Spot change ytd                                  -0.99%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation               35.36%\par  *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\p