\par\par \b \uc2 China gold and jewels exports spike points to hot money revival\b0\par\par\uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2014\par 22:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Economists suspect Sept export rise driven by speculation\par \par \uc2 * Previous speculative inflows prompted \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Flows could have been positioning for HK-Shanghai Stock Connect\par \par \uc2 * Yuan has been rallying since May\par \par \uc2 By Jake Spring\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Nov 7 (Reuters) - A sudden swell in China's exports of gold and jewellery may signal a resurgence of speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows through inflated trade receipts, raising the prospect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could again act to weaken the yuan and punish speculators.\par \par \uc2 Trade data this weekend will be closely watched for further evidence of illegal cross-border arbitrage, after September data showed a substantial gap between what China said it exported to Hong Kong and what Hong Kong said it imported from the mainland.\par \par \uc2 Inflated export receipts are one way to circumvent strict controls on bringing money into China. Speculators may be hoping to profit from a rising yuan or the imminent link-up between the Shanghai and Hong Kong stock exchanges.\par \par \uc2 "Precious metals contributed to the monthly jump and that is a very easy candidate for over-invoicing," said Chang Chun Hua, China economist at Nomura based in Hong Kong. "But, like commodity financing earlier this year which saw authorities crack down, this gap should shrink in the coming months."\par \par \uc2 Inflows of so-called hot money looking to profit from the steadily appreciating yuan prompted a surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 in February, which drove the yuan down by nearly 4 percent by the end of May, scaring off speculators but also punishing poorly hedged investors and companies.\par \par \uc2 If evidence that such inflows have resumed continues to mount, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be tempted to deliver another reminder that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a one-way bet.\par \par \uc2 Chinese exports to Hong Kong, where over-invoicing is typically most pronounced, outstripped Hong Kong's recorded imports from China by more than $13.5 billion in September, according to a note from Nomura on Tuesday, roughly double the average monthly gap for the previous eight months of the year.\par \par \uc2 Jewellery and precious metal exports jumped 678 percent year-on-year or $9.4 billion, potentially providing evidence of a rise in fake trade flows, economists at ANZ in Hong Kong said in a research note on Monday.\par \par \uc2 A media report last week said authorities were investigating suspected irregularities in the surge in precious metals exports.\par \par \uc2 China launched a crackdown on falsified invoicing in 2013, and in September the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said it had stamped out the practice after uncovering $10 billion worth of fake trades.\par \par \uc2 'A' SHARES LINK?\par \par \uc2 Most analysts expect gains in the yuan, which has risen around 2 percent since May, to be more modest this year than in 2013. But Chinese fixed-income yields remain higher than much of the region, and even modest appreciation will likely outstrip neighbouring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the end of quantitative easing pulls money back to dollar.\par \par \uc2 If speculative money is back, it could also be targeting "A shares" on the Shanghai stock market, otherwise restricted to domestic investors, to benefit from an expected bounce from the postponed launch of the Shanghai-Hong Kong Stock Connect.\par \par \uc2 Underscoring the demand for yuan assets, Hong Kong's quota under the Renminbi Qualified Institutional Investor programme, a legal means for foreigners to invest in A shares, was exhausted as of September, which may have driven some investors to use illegal routes.\par \par \uc2 Shanghai's A shares index rose 5.75 percent in September.\par \par \uc2 The Connect or "through train" scheme, which will allow global investors to trade Chinese shares via Hong Kong for the first time, did not launch as expected in October and may now be delayed for months.\par \par \uc2 "We may see overbilling cool down in the coming months as the stock link programme has not obtained approval according to HKEx's statement released on Oct. 17, while China's authorities could crack down on such activities," said Gao Qi, a Singapore-based trading strategist at RBS, in a note last week.\par \par \uc2 If money did flow in on the back of the exchange tie-up, China's October trade data release on Saturday may already begin to reveal a correction in the export-import gap with Hong Kong. But if the gap persists, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider action.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that the authorities, including the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , are closely monitoring possible resurrection of cross-border financial arbitrage and non-genuine trade activities," ANZ economists wrote in their note this week.\par \par \uc2 "We should pay attention to possible consequences of the discrepancy between Hong Kong and Mainland China's trade data."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Saikat Chatterjee; Editing by Pete Sweeney and Alex Richardson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-6T22:00:39.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141106eab600o2v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
