\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan dips on corporate dollar demand, could end the week flat\b0\par\par\uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2014\par 07:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Oct 24 (Reuters) - China's yuan inched lower\par against the dollar on Friday, pushed down by corporate dollar\par demand and a slightly lower \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  midpoint, traders said.\par     Spot yuan eased to 6.1203 per dollar by midday,\par 0.01 percent weaker than Thursday's close. At the midday level,\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was down 0.1 percent for the week. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 fixed its midpoint\par  at 6.1467, down 0.01 percent from Thursday's fix. The\par official rate was at a two-week low, reflecting the recent\par global strength of the U.S. dollar index. \par     The yuan has rallied since late September, hitting a\par seven-month high at 6.1172 per dollar on Wednesday. It has been\par buoyed by China's strong trade surpluses every month since May,\par which have increased dollar supply in the market.\par     "This round of rally is steady but slow, indicating the\par market is still divided on the direction of yuan," said a trader\par at a commercial bank in Shanghai. \par     For now, however, more investors are inclined to bet on\par appreciation as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems to step in to support the\par yuan, traders said. They believe the yuan is likely to hover\par around 6.12 in coming weeks, with the potential to rise above\par 6.10.\par     \par     CORPORATE DOLLAR DEMAND\par     Traders said corporate dollar demand has been on the rise\par this year after the yuan depreciated 3.4 percent in the first\par four months under the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's guidance and in line with \par the recent dollar strength in global markets.\par     In September, Chinese banks had a deficit of $7.88 billion\par in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  settlements for corporate clients, the first\par time since August 2012 there wasn't a surplus, according to data\par  the \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Administration of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (SAFE) released on\par Thursday.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  and commercial banks together purchased only\par $179.73 million worth of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a net basis in\par September, according to a Reuters calculation. \par     Both pieces of data contrast with China's big September\par trade surplus. This indicates companies are keeping more dollars\par on hand this year than in the past few, when the yuan was seeing\par one-way appreciation. One likely reason is concern over slowing\par global growth. \par     While the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  is guiding the yuan to stage more two-way\par trading this year, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also building mechanisms\par to eventually foster a more freely traded yuan, traders said.\par     In the latest move, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market will kick off\par direct trading between the yuan and the Singapore dollar \par next week. \par          \par The onshore spot yuan market at a glance: \par  Item                    Current      Previous     Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint           6.1467       6.1459       -0.01%\par                                                    \par  Spot yuan               6.1203       6.1195       -0.01%\par  Divergence from         -0.43%                    \par  midpoint* 