\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS- China's capital defences have sprung a major leak\b0\par\par\uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2014\par 05:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.) (Refiles to add link.)\par \par \uc2 By John Foley\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Oct 22 (Reuters Breakingviews) - China's strict capital controls are designed to shield the economy from flighty foreign money. In practice, they haven't stopped a tremendous build up of fickle cash from abroad. There's enough to create a serious nuisance if it starts to flow the other way.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  allows money to cross the border for trade or long-term investment, but generally frowns on short-term speculation. Nevertheless, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data suggests $725 billion of what might be called "hot money" has piled up since 2008. That's the combination of short-term funds Chinese residents have borrowed from foreign lenders, and securities they have sold to overseas investors, minus anything that was repaid or sold.\par \par \uc2 Graphic: China's foreign capital iceberg: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/cub33w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/cub33w}}}\par \par \uc2 The real quantum of hot money is undoubtedly greater. Some activity that passes as trade is actually cover for transferring speculative capital. Anomalies like the 34 percent increase in exports to Hong Kong in September suggest widespread shenanigans.\par \par \uc2 Most of the inflows have come because rates in China are so much higher than in Western economies. Add in the yuan's rise against the dollar over the years, and the returns for foreign investors have been too good to ignore.\par \par \uc2 The $725 billion pile is unlikely to trigger a balance of payments crisis - where a country is left without enough foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repay foreign debts or pay for crucial imports. Even if holders demanded their money back en masse, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $3.9 trillion of foreign reserves can satisfy all of their claims. A bigger risk is that an outflow leaves banks short of deposits which fund domestic loans. Even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected emergency funds, there's no guarantee that spooked banks would want to lend.\par \par \uc2 The mood music is already troubling. China's foreign reserves have fallen by $106 billion over the past three months, and local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits grew at 9.3 percent in September, their slowest rate on record. That suggests money is leaving China, although some will be due to tourism and relaxed rules on foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 The recent strengthening of the yuan suggests the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  wants to get speculative money flowing back in. It is also planning to inject money into banks directly. But it's not ideal when monetary policy becomes hostage to short-term capital flows. China's barriers have served it well, but its economy is more driven by what happens outside than planners might like to admit.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/johnsfoley"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/johnsfoley}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par CONTEXT NEWS\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves were $3.89 trillion at\par the end of September, an $81.1 billion decline from the previous\par month, and $106 billion less than the end of June. The decrease\par was the biggest reported since May 2012, and only the\par seventeenth time the balance has fallen since the beginning of\par 2000, according to data from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Administration of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign\par Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  accumulates reserves when it buys\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from residents, which enters the country principally\par through trade and investment. Buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is one way the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to affect the price of the\par yuan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deposits grew by 9.3 percent in September\par compared to the previous year, the lowest monthly rate of\par increase on record, based on Datastream figures going back to\par 1996. During the previous twelve months, the average reported\par growth rate was 12.5 percent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - China's trade surplus in September was $31 billion, more\par than double the previous year's figure. Exports increased by\par 15.3 percent, while imports were up 7 percent. The surplus was\par lower, though, than August's record $49.8 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Breakingviews TV: Watch out for hot money gone cold in\par China: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/1wt2He5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/1wt2He5}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 - Graphic: China's foreign capital iceberg: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/cub33w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/cub33w}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 - Reuters: China Sept data shows loan recovery but weak\par investment, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outflows cloud picture\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RELATED COLUMNS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A world apart\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New tricks\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 House of pain\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can\par click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Katrina Hamlin)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-10-25T03:41:41.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141022eaam003h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
