\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Close To Zero  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  In 3Q -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2014\par 14:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1202 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Chinese reserve figures for the third quarter show that over the last two quarters there has been close to zero \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Jens Nordvig at Nomura. "This is a massive change from the six months prior, when Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was running at more than $100 billion per quarter," he says. Adds it helps explain why the renminbi has been immune to EM weakness lately. "It also may have implications for global flow dynamics going forward, although it is hardly showing up in the price action in global bond markets yet." (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 1151 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The dollar will continue to benefit from global risk-off sentiment, combined with strong domestic data, says Vasileios Gkionakis at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . "The intensification of global risk aversion provided the yen and the Swiss franc with relief this past week, while the U.S. dollar weakened across the board," he says, but the attitude of markets is likely to change if, as he believes, incoming U.S. data on economic activity remain on the strong side. "Hence, although choppy price action may be with us for a few days, largely due to extended long USD positioning, the U.S. dollar is likely to benefit." (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Weaker oil prices could meant that Thailand saves $119 million each quarter, or nearly half a billion dollars a year, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  calculates. But a bigger determinant for the country's economic health is the value of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the general state of demand for the country's exports. A 10% depreciation in the value of the baht translates into a quarterly additional net trade surplus of $466 million. A ten percent rise in export volume to the rest of the world would potentially mean a quarterly net trade surplus of $230 million. "While these impact estimates could vary depending on the regression technique and data used, we are inclined to the view that the windfall gain brought about by soft oil prices on the current account remains modest compared to the impact of a weak Thai baht or shifts in external demand," Citi said in a research note to clients. Its year-end target for the baht is 32.70 baht to the dollar. (wilawan.watcharasakwet@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:30 EDT - It's obviously been a rollicking week, and investors appear to be looking to the end of it with a bit of a risk-on mentality. Stocks and oil are sharply higher while sovereign debt is retreating some. Where things are is by no means indication of how the day will pan out, but it certainly brightens the mood some. What may have already been brightening of late is consumer confidence--thanks to slumping gasoline prices. We'll see if that's shown up yet this month in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 University of Michigan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's survey in 2 1/2 hours. S&P 500 futures are up 26 and oil gains some 1.5% while gold ticks lower. The dollar is slightly lower, with the euro at $1.2820, and the 10-year yield has backed up to 2.18%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:28 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chilean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to continue with the reduction of its benchmark interest rate, after a Thursday reduction 25 bps to 3%, due to economy deceleration, says Edward Glossop Emerging Markets \f28 \uc2 \f2 Economist\f28 \uc2 \f2  at Capital Economics. "We expect further cuts over the next couple of quarters. We're sticking to our forecast for 50bp of cuts to 2.50% by mid-15 but there is scope for more reductions if the economic outlook deteriorates further," says Mr. Glossop. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:14 EDT - European equity markets rallied following signs that U.S. markets were finding a bottom. Helping investors wade back in are central bankers. Yesterday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 St Louis Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 James Bullard\f28 \uc2 \f2  suggested that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  could hold off ending its asset purchase program and today ECB Executive Board member Beno\u238\'00\'EEt Coeure said asset-backed security purchases would begin within days. But it's not clear that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cavalry is coming. Bullard's idea will surely be opposed by hawks, such as Philadelphia Fed President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Plosser\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and may also draw opposition from more centrist FOMC members. Within the ECB, Germany is resistant to aggressive action and now Austrian National Bank President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ewald Nowotny\f28 \uc2 \f2  has added his voice of caution. Still, the very idea that central banks are ready with monetary support is likely enough for a few bargain-hunters. (michael.casey@wsj.com and alen.mattich@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1114 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  warns that operating margins at the four big retailers in the U.K--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 J Sainsbury\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Asda and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wm Morrison\f28 \uc2 \f2 --will fall to an average of 2.5% over the next 12-18 months from around 3% now and they will have to cut prices to stem sales decline and slow a loss of market share. "Further price cuts could be particularly credit negative for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Morrison as their cost cutting and efficiency measures are unlikely to fully offset the negative impact of lower prices on their margins," says Sven Reinke, a vice president at the ratings firm. He adds that discount retailers, like \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aldi\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lidl\f28 \uc2 \f2 , pose a particular threat to the "Big Four". (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1108 GMT Given the current risk-off market environment, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects lower activity in the high-yield space in the fourth quarter. "With high-yield markets remaining more fragile into 4Q, we expect some issuers, especially those opportunistically seeking to reprice, will opt to wait on the sidelines to see how the market environment develops," says Peter Firth, in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  European leveraged finance team. However, he adds, "if yields continue their September trend this may prove to be the wrong choice for some issuers." (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1107 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Vontobel Asset Management is launching a new bond fund, in response, it says, to the challenge of reaching attractive returns via traditional bond portfolios these days. The fund will be called Vontobel Bond Fund Global Aggegate and will allow the company's head of fixed income, Herve Hanoune, to invest in sovereign, corporate, high-yield and Emerging Market debt, as well as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vontobel\f28 \uc2 \f2  managed a total of CHF163 billion at end-June, CHF10 billion of that in various fixed-rated bond products. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha). \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1059 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists trim their GDP forecasts for Greece on the back of recent data. They now expect GDP growth of 0.4% in 2014, from 0.5% before, and 1.4% in 2015 from 1.7% before. "We still see little room for Greece to surprise to the upside; in our view, recent data imply heightened downside risks" say the economists. While tourism and car sales are the only indicators still showing some strength, "industrial production in seasonally adjusted terms fell to a new low, while total exports, which have still not picked up, are below second quarter levels," they write in a note. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0000 GMT (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--Resistance at Y171.73 on GBP/JPY is temporarily blocking the recovery from Tuesday's six-month low at Y168.02. The origin of the rally at Y168.02 is an example of how the influential 260-day moving average can stall a downtrend, and the structure of the rally indicates further gains to Y172 and Y172.50 are on offer. The Y172.50 target lies close to the 38.2% Fibonacci retracement of the four-week decline from Y180.73, and a fresh wave of GBP bull pressure would be required to force a break through Y172.50. A reversal below the session low at Y170.20 would question the bullish outlook. GBP/JPY is at Y171.49.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Francis Bray, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\f28 \uc2 \f2  Analyst For Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1040 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--After rather dovish comments from \f28 \uc2 \f2 James Bullard\f28 \uc2 \f2 - usually the hawk of St. Louis- markets will be very keen to hear what Fed Chair Yellen thinks of the idea that QE might last a bit longer. However, they may have to wait. Ms. Yellen is speaking today, in Boston, but her subject is apparently "Economic Opportunity." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks this may offer scant scope for digression into monetary policy or the state of the economy. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 17, 2014 08:02 ET (12:02 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141017eaah001de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
