\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan eases, loses 0.1 pct on week\b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2014\par 06:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Oct 17 (Reuters) - China's yuan edged down\par slightly against dollar on Friday, pushed lower by corporate\par demand for dollars and after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a weaker\par midpoint, traders said. \par     Spot yuan eased to 6.1246 per dollar by midday,\par 0.02 percent weaker than Thursday's close, after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's\par Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 fixed its official midpoint at\par 6.1407, down 0.02 percent from Thursday's fix. \par     For the week, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is set to drop 0.1 percent if it\par ends at the midday level. \par     "Chinese companies and banks are more inclined to keep more\par dollars on hand of late due to concerns over China's economic\par outlook," said a trader at an Asian bank in Shanghai.    \par     China's trade performance posted a rebound in September,\par with both export and import data beating expectations.\par  \par     However, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell slightly to\par $3.89 trillion at the end of September from $3.99 trillion at\par the end of June, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data showed on Thursday.\par  \par     Some analysts say the fall in reserves may have partly\par reflected some "hot money" outflows from China. \par     "I need more evidence to decide whether there are outflows\par or not. But if the outflows are true, the authorities may take\par some measures to boost the yuan's value in the longer term,"\par said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai.\par     He said that views in the market were split over what\par direction the yuan would take.\par     He also forecast the yuan will be relatively stable ahead of\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Communist Party\f28 \uc2 \f2  of China's fourth plenary session later this\par month.\par          \par The onshore spot yuan market at a glance: \par  Item                     Current      Previous     Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint            6.1407       6.1395       -0.02%\par                                                     \par  Spot yuan                6.1246       6.1231       -0.02%\par  Divergence from          -0.26%                    \par  midpoint*                                          \par  Spot change ytd                                    -1.15%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation                 35.14%\par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par     \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:\par  Instrument                      Current      Difference\par                                               from\par                                               onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan          *   6.1438       -0.31%\par  Offshore non-deliverable        6.2530       -1.80%\par  forwards               **                    \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par     \par     >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>> \par \par     FACTBOX\par     - Yuan and capital account reforms and pilot programmes     \par  \par     \par     RECENT DEVELOPMENTS\par     - \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 