\par\par \b \uc2 Treasury Calls on Core Eurozone Economies to Do More for Growth -3-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley   \par \uc2 1,118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2014\par 03:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON--The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  criticized Germany and other economies with trade surpluses for relying too heavily on exports to aid growth, and called for bolder efforts to boost consumer spending and support a flagging global recovery.\par \par \uc2 In Treasury's semiannual report to \uc2 Congress\uc2  on the exchange-rate policies of major trading partners, the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration also took China and South Korea to task over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies that it says undermine growth prospects in other economies.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury also said that while Japan needs to carefully calibrate its budget belt-tightening to avoid depressing growth, it can't rely on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's stimulus to support its economy. The BOJ's easy-money policies, which weaken the yen, have riled U.S. auto makers.\par \par \uc2 "Global growth and job creation continue to disappoint, due principally to chronically weak global demand," the Treasury Department said in a statement accompanying the report, which is mandated by \uc2 Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Treasury often uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report as a diplomatic tool to censure major trading partners for policies deemed potentially damaging to the U.S. economy. The last time the U.S. labeled a trading partner a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator--a technical term that can trigger U.S. trade sanctions--was the Clinton administration's rebuke of China in 1994. The \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration has used the report in recent years to criticize China, Germany, Japan and South Korea for their economic policies that keep exports cheap at the expense of other countries.\par \par \uc2 "The report's focus on current-account surpluses in countries such as Germany and deflationary risks in Europe and Japan points to U.S. concerns about the sustainability of its own recovery if the U.S. is the sole engine of global growth," said \uc2 Eswar Prasad\uc2 , a senior fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  and former IMF economist.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's report comes after the dollar recently topped five-year highs against a basket of other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . U.S. officials worry that weakness overseas and the strong greenback could undermine an American recovery that has been showing signs of gathering steam. \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials last week said they were concerned the dollar's strength could weigh on growth and keep a lid on inflation that remains below their target.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the dollar has retreated. But the broader drivers of the dollar's strength remain in place. U.S. officials worry countries will fall back on policies that weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than making politically tough economic overhauls that can boost domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 "In light of the weakening global backdrop, it is especially important that all \uc2 G-7\uc2  and G-20 countries adhere steadfastly to their exchange rate commitments," Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  said in remarks at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  meetings in Washington last weekend, referring to the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  industrialized nations and the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  largest economies.\par \par \uc2 But despite the dollar's recent gains, the Treasury's report used a more measured tone than its other recent reports. A year ago, the department blasted Germany for its economic policies, blaming the eurozone powerhouse for dragging down the regional and global economies.\par \par \uc2 Efforts by some of the hardest-hit countries in the eurozone to trim budgets and cut dangerously high debt levels have "not been matched with accommodative policies in the euro area core," Treasury said in the report. Mr. Lew and his top lieutenants have pressed Germany to expand its budget to boost domestic demand and allow the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the assets of weak member states to help stimulate growth.\par \par \uc2 Germany has resisted calls to spend more to stave off another potential recession in Europe. Last week, Finance Minister \uc2 Wolfgang Sch\u228\'00\'E4uble\uc2  said policy makers globally must solve current problems "without falling back to the mistakes that created the crisis." On Tuesday, economics minister Sigmar Gabriel said there was no cause for Berlin to change its strategy. "We're not going to help the German economy with some flashes in the pan and more debt," Mr. Gabriel said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  last week downgraded its outlook for global growth, warning that the Eurozone faces a 40% chance of re-entering a recession and citing a broad slowdown in several major emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The administration praised China's gradual appreciation of the yuan in recent years, but said the yuan remains "significantly undervalued." Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last year raised questions about the government's commitment to move toward a fully market-determined exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 China's growth rate is falling amid a weaker global outlook and as some of its economic overhauls weigh on output. Beijing has appreciated the yuan nearly 15%, accounting for inflation, since 2010, but restarted exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last year amid slowing growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 In April, the administration criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan depreciation as an "unprecedented" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , renewing long-running exchange-rate frictions between the world's two largest economies. But after high-level talks in Beijing over the summer, Mr. Lew said Chinese official committed to reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "As \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reform strategy proceeds...it is important that domestic consumption, and not a renewed dependence on external demand, drive China's growth," the Treasury report said.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department warned that Tokyo needs to complete its planned economic overhaul, saying monetary policy can't offset budget tightening "nor can it substitute for necessary structural reforms that raise trend growth and domestic demand," the report said.\par \par \uc2 Treasury last year pressed Japan into signing a \uc2 G-7\uc2  pact to avoid targeting exchange rates after government officials in Tokyo talked about weakening the yen to give the economy a boost. Washington wanted to prevent a destabilizing global round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations as other countries retaliated by pushing down the values of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since then, administration officials have been fairly sanguine about the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's easy-money policies that have helped push down the value of the yen.\par \par \uc2 Treasury also censured South Korea's exchange rate policies, reiterating warnings that Seoul should limit \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "only to the exceptional circumstance of disorderly market conditions." The department estimated Korean officials spent more $14 billion between May and July keeping a lid on the won's value. A recent rise in Korea's won in recent months prompted Seoul to signal in July it could resume \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter the deterioration in the country's export competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the Chinese, Korean and Japanese embassies weren't immediately available to comment.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ian Talley at ian.talley@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-10-14 0148GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020141016eaag0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
