\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 4-China Sept export, import growth beats expectations\b0\par\par\uc2 922 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2014\par 04:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds fresh comment)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Oct 13 (Reuters) - China's exports rose 15.3 percent in September from a year earlier, quickening from a 9.4 percent rise in August and well above market expectations, data showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Imports rose 7 percent in September from a year earlier, versus a 2.7 percent decline predicted by economists. That produced a trade surplus of $31 billion for the month, down from August's $49.8 billion.\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ KEY POINTS Exports +15.3 y/y (f'cast +11.8 pct, prev month +9.4 pct) Imports +7 pct y/y (f'cast -2.7 pct, prev month -2.4 pct) Trade surplus $31.1 bln (f'cast surplus $41 bln, prev month $49.8 bln) COMMENTARY: ZHOU HAO, \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2  AT ANZ, SHANGHAI: "While recent data in the U.S. continue to remain upbeat, we believe that China's export growth could moderate somewhat in Q4 largely due to base effects. "Notably, China's exports to Hong Kong accelerated to 34 percent y/y in September, versus -2.1 percent in the prior month, coincident with the renewed RMB appreciation in the past few months. This triggers concerns that speculative trade flows to ride on RMB appreciation could have reemerged. Thus, the trade developments between Hong Kong and mainland China needs to be closely monitored. "The stronger-than-expected imports appear to be somewhat inconsistent with the sluggish domestic demand, but suggest that the commodity traders could have taken advantage of the low commodity prices in the past month. "While the huge trade surplus in Q3 will likely provide some positive impetus, the decelerating momentum in domestic demand actually points to downside risk to Q3 GDP growth. We expect that China's GDP will only rise 7.1 percent y/y in Q3, the slowest pace since the global financial crisis." "Meanwhile, the large trade surplus will continue to add appreciation pressure on RMB. Notably, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase data suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has significantly reduced the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since June. The reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , plus the recent USD strengthening, will likely bring increasing volatility to RMB." ANNETTE BEACHER, \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2  AT \uc2 TD SECURITIES\uc2 , SINGAPORE: "At face value it's an Aussie dollar, Kiwi dollar and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  favorable report, if China is actually importing and trading that does tell you the health of the economy may not be as bad as currently perceived. "In terms of monetary policy, per se, I think it's neutral."\par \par \uc2 LOUIS KUIJS\uc2 , CHIEF CHINA \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2  AT \uc2 ROYAL BANK OF SCOTLAND\uc2 , HONG KONG: "This seems to be very strong export growth (though) underlying momentum in export growth is a bit less impressive than the headline number suggests because we had a very weak base 12 months ago. "This is still robust export momentum if you look at sequentially month on month; I'm still a little bit hesitant in becoming very bullish on export growth, simply looking at the state of the global economy. "The data shows decent export growth and decent import growth, that probably will strengthen the senior leadership's current stance on macro policy, which is to accept some of downward pressure we are seeing. Because they think the economy's overall momentum is not that bad and the labour market is holding up, this will strengthen the policymakers resolve in not resorting to additional stimulus measures."\par \par \uc2 HAIBIN ZHU, CHIEF CHINA \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2  AT \uc2 JPMORGAN\uc2 , HONG KONG: "The big implication is that imports are much stronger, so no matter what the reason I think it will cool off a bit of the concern about domestic weakness bringing down this activity. Zhu also said a lower trade surplus will reduce some of the appreciation pressure on the renminbi \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Zhu said the data is unlikely to change the government's monetary policy and he expects China to continue with unconventional policy measures that are more targeted to stimulate the economy, rather than resorting to wholesale stimulus. "One month ago the \uc2 State Council\uc2  issued some new policy to support import activity, so it might be that the policy is starting to take effect," Zhu said, although he said he needed more time to analyse the reasons behind the improvement in imports. BACKGROUND -- China's government is targeting total trade growth of 7.5 percent this year. Total trade grew 7.6 percent in 2013, below the official target of 8 percent. -- \uc2 China's Commerce Ministry\uc2  has pledged to maintain steady trade growth in 2014 and further balance its trade structure by increasing imports of raw materials and energy products. -- China's exports have been sluggish for much of this year but recently began showing signs of life thanks to firmer global demand and government incentives for exporters. -- Imports have also been weak, highlighting sluggish domestic demand, but recent data has pointed to some signs of stabilisation. -- Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  said late last week that China will avoid a hard landing despite worries over a slowdown in the world's number-two economy and a flagging real-estate market. He also said he was confident the economy would continue to grow at a "medium to high tempo", forecasting growth of about 7.5 percent this year despite turbulence in the world economy. -- Many economists believe more stimulus may be needed if economic growth is to meet the official target. (Reporting by China economics team; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-10-13T03:55:59.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141013eaad001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
