\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei\uc2  Down 1.5% Midday; \uc2 Sumitomo Corp\uc2 . Drags Sector Lower -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2014\par 05:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0341 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nikkei\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 1.5% at 16070.90 midday, as the dollar continues to soften (USD/JPY now at 109.30), inviting profit-taking. "The Japan industrial production and related data are not factors in today's trading activity, but investors are nevertheless reluctant to take any new positions ahead of the upcoming dual Hong Kong and China market holidays," says an equity trading director at a European brokerage. "It will affect basket trades, in which Japan trading positions are often included," he says. The Hong Kong Stock Exchange will be closed Oct. 1-2, while the mainland will be closed Oct. 1-7. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumitomo Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (8053.TO) is off 13% at Y1,203 after announcing large losses on a U.S. shale oil project, dragging its sector lower. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The lowest accepted price at the auction of 2-year JGBs due at 0345 GMT is likely to be around 100.04, traders say. The latest No. 345 issue carries a 0.1% coupon, unchanged from the last issue. Market participants say the auction is likely to go smoothly on the back of the BOJ's massive bond purchase program, with little effect expected on the secondary market. Lead December JGB futures are up 0.09 at 145.92 at the midday close. (Eleanor.Warnock@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0329 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/USD may break below its next downside support of 1.2600, depending on eurozone and U.S. economic data later today, says Junichi Ishikawa, market analyst at IG Securities. A possible low reading in the eurozone \f28 \uc2 \f2 CPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  data again would suggest ECB easing steps ahead. And an upbeat reading in U.S. economic data later in the day, such as the S&P/Case-Shiller home price index, may give investors a clearer signal of U.S.-euro zone policy divergence. Ishikawa says even with a falloff in \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields overnight, the EUR/USD "failed to try even the 5-day moving average" (around 1.2720) and then turned down, underscoring how the market's expectations for future USD strength remains pervasive. The EUR/USD is at 1.2694. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Crude-oil futures remain largely rangebound in Asian trade after the release of Chinese manufacturing data. \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's China Manufacturing PMI was unchanged at a final reading of 50.2 in September from August. "We expect rising WTI prices to start decreasing its momentum with a weaker-than-expected China manufacturing PMI," analyst Howie Lee at Phillip Futures says. He says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  WTI crude prices rose 2.9% after preliminary U.S. manufacturing PMI was released last week, and as markets anticipate a positive final reading Wednesday. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude is down 12 cents at $94.47/bbl, Brent crude is up 6 cents at $97.26/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0301 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Malaysian ringgit has lost 3.7% this month--mainly due to external risk factors--but could decline further versus the U.S. dollar, the bullish USD/MYR weekly chart suggests. USD/MYR entered the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel two weeks ago and will be above the Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone contingent on a Friday close above 3.2570. This could motivate speculators to bet on the U.S. dollar rising to the round-figure trading barrier at 3.3000, a level last seen in March. The likelihood of geopolitical turmoil escalating has been driving investors away from risky assets and toward the safe haven U.S. dollar. USD/MYR is now 3.2725 versus its Monday close of 3.2770. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0251 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's baht recovers to 32.35 from Monday's two-month low of 32.41 versus the U.S. dollar but could weaken again in the near term, the daily chart suggests. The daily Bollinger uptrend channel remains in play if USD/THB stays above 32.30 by the end of Tuesday. The immediate target for the pair is the 32.41 top of the uptrend channel, followed by the psychological resistance level at 32.50. The baht is under pressure after weak August trade data released Monday. A sharp drop in exports has tamed analysts' expectations for Thailand's third-quarter growth results. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thai finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  has also downgraded its outlook, saying that the economy is unlikely to grow 2.0% for the year. USD/THB is now 32.37 versus its Monday close of 32.39. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Australia's equities market is flat at midday as bank stocks stabilize after several big lenders fell 10% or more in recent weeks from their highs this year. Michael McCarthy, chief market strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 CMC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Markets, says it's another difficult day for investors with negative regional sentiment flowing from protests in Hong Kong countered by a strengthening of some commodities prices that has brought some support. "The question for today, and for the week, is at what point concern gives way to a much improved investment case," he says. The S&P/ASX is up 3.7 points at 5267.9. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia & New Zealand Banking\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ANZ.AU) is up 0.9% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  (WBC.AU) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Australia Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (NAB.AU) are each up 0.3%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  suggests valuation support is increasing for bank stocks that have over the last three months underperformed both the wider Australian market and international banks. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RobbMStewart)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The yuan extends losses after it recorded its biggest daily drop in more than three months Monday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains weak in the offshore market and month-end corporate dollar purchases continue. The yuan is at 6.1523 to a dollar, versus the 6.1501 level seen at Monday's close. The central parity is set at 6.1525 versus 6.1539 Monday. "It's good that the market will closed for a week from Wednesday--we will see a more clear picture about Hong Kong when we come back," says a Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank, adding that the dollar-yuan may fall below the 6.1400 level if the protests end during the holiday. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the yuan's fall accelerates after the holiday, he adds. The USD/CNH, or the offshore yuan, is at 6.1747 versus 6.1678 late Monday. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY non-deliverable forwards are down at 6.2665/6.2690 from 6.2680/6.2734 late Monday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0244 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] An unexpected rise in Hong Kong's retail sales data for August may be short-lived as throngs of protesters crowd the city's streets and shopping areas, putting pressure on GDP growth rates, says Nomura. Hong Kong's retail sales unexpectedly rose in August for the first time in seven months, gaining 3.4% year-on-year to HK$40 billion, compared to expectations of a 2% decline. Higher sales of alcohol and tobacco, motor vehicles and clothing offset declining sales of electronics and luxury goods. "For September, electronics sales should rebound due to the new iPhone6 launches in Hong Kong, but recent political unrest may have a negative impact on retail sales," the bank says. "Overall, we see downside risks to our Hong Kong GDP growth forecast (2.5% in 2014 and 3.0% in 2015) owing to a potential housing market correction and political unrest." (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/TWD is lower, weighed by selling by exporters around 30.470, says a local trader. He says the market is calmer after Monday's active trading dominated by foreign investor buying, but weaker local stocks are still supporting the USD. The pair is unlikely rise above 30.480 due to month-end exporter sales, the trader says. The pair is at 30.444, below the 30.480 it traded at before the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday, which lifted it to 30.502--the highest onshore closing level in almost six months. The Taiex is down 0.9%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China shares are slightly lower, weighed by signs of the fragility of the economic recovery, say analysts. The HSBC China Manufacturing PMI has been revised down to a final reading of 50.2 from a flash of 50.5, but is unchanged from the August reading. The revision may indicate a bumpy road to economic recovery, says Zhou Lin, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Huatai Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who tips the market to consolidate at the last trading day ahead of a weeklong holiday which starts October 1. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 0.1% at 2356.58, and is tipped to trade around 2350. Defense plays succumb to profit taking after a surge while coal miners gain on hopes of supportive measures from the government. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.3% at 1328.80. (amy.li@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Emerging markets saw $18 billion flow in, during September, according to monthly data from the International Institute of Finance. While that's slightly higher than August's sharply lower $12 billion, analysts noted that flows remained subdued. Indian and Mexican debt markets and the Brazilian stock market saw "significant inflows," while South Africa, Turkey and Indonesia saw investors pulling money out. "Investor concerns about \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  exit came back to the fore in September, weighing on EM portfolio flows," they wrote, adding that risk sentiment rose towards the end of September. According to the date, equity inflows slowed while debt flows picked up.(anjani.trivedi@wsj.com; twitter: @anjani_trivedi) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 29, 2014 23:41 ET (03:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140930ea9u0008q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
