\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan climbs to one-week highs, but upside seen limited\b0\par\par\uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2014\par 05:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Sept 26 (Reuters) - China's yuan climbed to a\par one-week high against the dollar on Friday, shrugging off\par another weaker midpoint set by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to reflect\par strength of the global dollar.\par     Spot yuan changed hands at 6.1352 per dollar near\par midday, up 0.02 percent from Thursday's close at 6.1363. It hit\par an intra-day high of 6.1324 in the morning trade.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 fixed the official\par midpoint at 6.1508, down 0.02 percent from the previous day's\par 6.1497. It was the lowest level in more than two weeks.\par     The dollar hit a four-year high as the yield difference\par between U.S. and German bonds widened the most in nearly 15\par years on Thursday. \par     The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading in very tight ranges most\par of the week and it is set to rise less than 0.1 percent for the\par week.\par     Some analysts believe the yuan is under further pressure to\par be weakened given China's sluggish fundamentals after its\par factory output grew at the slowest pace in nearly six years in\par August.\par     "We do see a modest renminbi depreciation against the dollar\par in light of a weakening Chinese economy and a strengthening\par dollar," said Wang Tao, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Hong Kong. \par     Wang expected the yuan to be around 6.20 by end-2014 and\par 6.35 by end-2015.\par \par The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:          \par  Item                        Current    Previous     Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 