\par\par \b \uc2 Grand Central: \uc2 Fed\uc2  Likely to Still See Jobs Slack as "Significant"\b0\par\par\uc2 2,289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2014\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal's Daily Report on Global Central Banks for Wednesday, September 17, 2014:\par \par \uc2 Sign up for the newsletter.\par \par \uc2 Portions of this newsletter were included in our Special Fed Preview edition sent out Tuesday afternoon. This updates that version with additional news from around the world.\par \par \uc2 Highlights \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Da Costa' Take: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  Likely to Still See Jobs Slack as "Significant" \par  \par  \par    -- Hilsenrath Analysis: The Case for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Tweak but Keep \par       "Considerable" Period For Now \par  \par  \par    -- Five Things to Watch Out of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  Meeting \par  \par  \par    -- China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Injects $81 Billion Into Top Banks to Counter \par       Slowdown \par  \par  \par    -- BOE Dissenters Fail to Attract Support \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DA COSTA'S TAKE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 FED\f28 \uc2 \f2  LIKELY TO STILL SEE JOBS SLACK AS "SIGNIFICANT"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It has been a busy week for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  Kremlinology as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers debate the likelihood of changes to key phrases in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The more closely-watched debate is over whether the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  will drop its pledge to hold interest rates very low for a "considerable time" after the bond-buying program ends in October. We believe it will not.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another potential change under discussion is the use of the word "significant" to characterize the underutilization of labor market resources -- i.e. workers not being employed to their full potential. St. Louis Fed President \f28 \uc2 \f2 James Bullard\f28 \uc2 \f2  says he argued against inserting the word back in the July meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 How have things changed on the labor slack front since then? The data have been at best mixed. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Labor Department\f28 \uc2 \f2  reported a 6.1% unemployment rate in August, unchanged since June. Job growth disappointed, breaking a recent streak of monthly gains over 200,000, while real wages have shown little sign of budging. But on the positive side, a broad measure of underemployment highlighted by top policy makers known as the U-6 rate dipped to 12.0% in August from 12.2% in July. Still, that's probably not enough to make what would likely be a market-rattling change in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Pedro Nicolaci da Costa\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MORNING MINUTES: KEY DEVELOPMENTS AROUND THE WORLD\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hilsenrath Analysis: The Case for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Tweak but Keep "Considerable" Period For Now. In a live video \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spreecast\f28 \uc2 \f2  Tuesday, the Journal's Jon Hilsenrath explained why he thought the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  would stick with, but qualify, an important phrase in its policy statement Wednesday which assures near-zero interest rates for a "considerable time." He wrote in a subsequent blogpost, "Ms. Yellen's most logical next step, to my mind, would be to stick for the time being to what she's been saying, which is that rates will stay low for a "considerable time" with the strong qualification that this could change if the job market keeps improving quickly."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ICYMI: Here are our favorite excerpts from the video:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hilsenrath: How the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  Could Tweak 'Considerable Time'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Where the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sees the Economy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asset bubbles and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If you can't get enough, here is entire video\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Five Things to Watch Out of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  Meeting. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  concludes a two-day policy meeting Wednesday afternoon with a statement, new economic projections and Ms. Yellen's press conference. The global audience will be scouring it all for new clues about when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  is likely to start raising short-term interest rates from near zero.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Reverse Repo Gains Are A Negative For European Short-term Assets. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a report Tuesday that much of the money that flows into what the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  calls its overnight reverse repurchase agreement facility appears to be driven by large short-term investment funds taking that same cash out of European short-term assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  Loans Draw Fed Scrutiny. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse Group AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  is under fire from U.S. regulators over concerns the bank is not heeding \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  warnings to stop financing risky corporate takeover deals, according to a person familiar with the matter. The Swiss bank in recent weeks received a letter from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  demanding it immediately address problems with its underwriting and distribution of "leveraged loans." The letter found problems with the bank's adherence to guidance issued last year warning banks to avoid deals that included too much debt or too few protections for the lenders in case of a default, according to the person familiar with the document.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors See Fed, ECB Policy Paths Diverging. Almost half of investors expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  to hike rates in the second quarter of 2015, up from 38% in August, according to the latest \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  monthly survey of 202 investors. By contrast, 42% percent of the panel expects the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to start quantitative easing by the end of 2014, up from 32% a month ago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Required Reading: Catch Up on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  Before Its Pronouncements. What will the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  announce out of its two-day policy meeting? What might Chairwoman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  say at her press conference? Gear up for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  decision and more with the Journal's recent coverage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Injects $81 Billion Into Top Banks to Counter Slowdown. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is injecting 500 billion yuan ($81 billion) into the country's five major state-owned banks as it moves to counter slower-than-expected growth in the world's No. 2 economy, \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par according to a senior Chinese banking executive.   Economists React . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Five Questions About ECB's Lending Program. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will announce Thursday how much banks borrowed in new four-year ECB loans aimed at boosting private-sector lending. The program, called Targeted Longer-Term Refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , or TLTRO, was part of a June stimulus package that included interest-rate cuts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB's Mersch: Securitized Assets Revival Could Boost Financial Institutions. A member of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Executive Board has suggested that a revival of the market for securitized assets in the eurozone could improve financial institutions' access to collateral. Yves Mersch said in a speech on Wednesday that there remains "a sufficient buffer of high-quality assets available for market participants," but added a word of caution - Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Eurozone Inflation Revised Higher. The annual rate of inflation in the 18 countries that use the euro was unchanged at 0.4% in August, as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's statistics agency revised a previous estimate that recorded a decline to 0.3%. However, the annual rate of inflation in the eurozone remained at the lowest level since Oct 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOE Dissenters Fail to Attract Support. Two officials at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  who first pushed for an immediate rise in interest rates in August failed to attract additional support at the Monetary Policy Committee's September meeting, according to minutes released Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada's Poloz Says Shift to Export-Led Growth Underway. Bank of Canada Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Stephen Poloz\f28 \uc2 \f2  said he is more confident that a desired shift toward an economy powered by exports and business investment is underway, as data indicate a lower Canadian dollar is giving a boost to sales abroad of non-energy goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Floating Breakwater is New Metaphor from BoC Chief. Mr. Poloz is fond of metaphors. Spaghetti sauce, airplanes and dogs on leashes have all made an appearance in his speeches as he used such analogies to illustrate difficult economic concepts. Tuesday's metaphor was floating breakwater. On the merits of a flexible exchange rate, he said, "It's like a floating breakwater across the mouth of a harbor that rises and falls with the tides, but absorbs the waves to help keep the water in the harbor calm." --Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Fears Weak Oil Price Could Threaten Economy. The Bank of Russia said Tuesday that the ruble and the Russian economy could be hit by sliding prices for commodities--the country's key exports--if oil prices fail to recover in the midterm. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also said it introduced one-day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps to help banks manage short-term liquidity. Russian banks, unable to borrow abroad, are experiencing a shortage of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand Keeps Rates Unchanged\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its benchmark interest rate unchanged at its latest monetary policy meeting Wednesday, citing the need to support the nation's economic recovery. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Monetary Policy Committee voted unanimously to maintain its policy rate at 2.0%. It last cut the rate in March, by 25 basis points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some Expect More Rate Cuts in Denmark. Denmark sold two-year debt with a negative yield at an auction Tuesday for the first time in more than two years, amid expectations that the country's recent interest rate cut will be followed by more.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary Eyes Long Period of Low Rates. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is working to cut the country's international debt exposure to avoid having to increase interest rates if emerging-market turmoil resurfaces, said Adam Balog, deputy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor , in an interview. The bank two years ago embarked on an unprecedented cycle of rate cuts, and it now hopes to keep borrowing costs low for a long time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Riksbank Meeting Minutes Strike Dovish Tone. The minutes of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swedish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting on Sept. 3 had a dovish feel with a number of rate setters emphasizing that easier policy remains an option if inflation fails to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Riksbank left its main repurchase, or repo, rate at 0.25% two weeks ago having cut it by 0.5 percentage points in July. The July reduction was justified by the bank's aim to push up inflation to a 2% target from levels close to zero at present - Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SNB Preview: Negative Rates, or Wait and See? If the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Scotland were not enough to hold your interest, fear not: Thursday brings a quarterly rates decision from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Should you care? Well, the Swiss franc has been trading in tight ranges against its neighboring euro since the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pegged the two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but a change in the SNB's stance could have ripple effects on all major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as central banks's policies divergence widens. So, yes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17 Sep 2014 06:45 ET \par Grand Central: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  Likely to Still See Jobs Slack -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia Interest Rate Increase Looking More Likely. Malaysia could be set for another interest rate increase, economists said, after data late Wednesday showed prices unexpectedly accelerated last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GRAPHIC CONTENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With economic growth stalled, China's leaders have made clear the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's priority is stimulating the economy -- even at the risk of delaying or reversing efforts at financial reform. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  "is making a heroic but schizophrenic effort to maintain momentum on financial market reforms while alleviating political pressure to support growth by using targeted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cornell University\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eswar Prasad\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who tracked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FORWARD GUIDANCE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  releases a policy statement and updated economic projections at 2 p.m. EDT. Chairwoman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  holds a press conference at 2:30 p.m. EDT. Follow the decision and press conference on our live blog on wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RESEARCH\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Making Sense of Dissents: A History of FOMC Dissents. Dissenting votes were fairly rare in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's early years, but became more frequent starting in the late 1950s and have averaged nearly one per meeting since the financial crisis, Daniel L. Thornton and David C. Wheelock wrote in a review article from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 St. Louis Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Like other researchers, we find that over the FOMC's history, Federal Reserve Bank presidents more often dissented in favor of tighter policy than easier policy, whereas a majority of dissents by Federal Reserve governors were in favor of easier policy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMENTARY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market volatility hit record lows earlier this year, but is now "starting to edge higher in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Mark Gilbert writes in a Bloomberg View column. "The slow return of volatility is good news for those concerned about financial whiplash if yield-chasing investors all rush for the exit at once."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BASIS POINTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Chief executives downgraded their optimism about the U.S. economy, saying they expect to slow both hiring and capital investments in the second half of the year, according to the Business Roundtable's third-quarter survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The median U.S. annual household income--the level at which half are above and half below--rose 0.3% in 2013, or a total of $180, to an inflation-adjusted $51,939, the Census Bureau's latest snapshot of U.S. living standards showed Tuesday. That leaves incomes around 8% below their level of 2007, when the recession started.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -The hot new download for your iPad: "The Fed Museum," which the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chicago Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  describes as an "education-focused app allows users access to a wide range of information about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , economic history, supervision and regulation, the structure of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and the Federal Open Market Committee."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SIGN UP: Grand Central, straight to your inbox.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FEEDBACK LOOP: Send us your tips, suggestions and feedback. Write to: Jon.Hilsenrath@wsj.com; Victoria.McGrane@wsj.com; Pedro.daCosta@wsj.com; Michael.Derby@wsj.com; Nell.Henderson@wsj.com; Brian.Blackstone@wsj.com; jason.douglas@wsj.com; Ben.Leubsdorf@wsj.com; Paul.Hannon@wsj.com; Jacob.Schlesinger@wsj.com sarah.portlock@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Follow us on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @WSJCentralBanks, @NHendersonWSJ, @pdacosta, @Blackstonebrian, @PaulHannon29, @michaelsderby, @vgmac, @wsj_douglasj, @BenLeubsdorf, @JMSchels, @MargitFeher @NirmalaMenon @ToddBuell @sarahportlock \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 17, 2014 06:45 ET (10:45 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140917ea9h0018r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
