\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan slips, but set to rise slightly for the week\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2014\par 05:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Sept 12 (Reuters) - China's yuan slipped against\par the dollar on Friday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixed a weaker\par midpoint for a second consecutive day, but it was set to rise\par slightly for the week as market sentiment on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par improves.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  fixed the yuan's midpoint at\par 6.1468 per dollar prior to market open, 0.02 percent weaker than\par the previous day's 6.1453. \par     The spot market changed hands at 6.1325 near midday,\par weakening 0.04 percent from Wednesday's close at 6.1300.        \par     "The RMB should stay strong and there is a risk that it\par overshoots our current year-end forecast of 6.14 against the\par USD," said Ju Wang, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been a top performer since June,\par not only against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  struggling with low inflation\par and yields, but also against most emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Long positions in the yuan rose to their largest level since\par late January, according to a survey of 12 analysts conducted\par between Tuesday and Thursday. \par \par The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:         \par  Item                       Current    Previous     Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint              6.1468     