\par\par \b \uc2 VietinBank\uc2  Signs for Loan Expecting Stronger Economy -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2014\par 05:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0303 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Vietnam Joint Stock Commercial Bank for Industry and Trade\uc2  (CTG.VH), or \uc2 VietinBank\uc2 , signed an agreement for a $50 million syndicated loan from four foreign banks led by Cathay United Bank as it is building up funds in anticipation of a faster economic recovery, \uc2 VietinBank\uc2  CEO Le Duc Tho says. "The economic recovery is expected to accelerate and demand for loans will increase," Tho says, adding that the loan deal, signed Wednesday, shows that foreign investors have confidence in his bank. He adds that \uc2 VietinBank\uc2  is also keeping an eye on the possibility of selling dollar-denominated bonds in the international market. (trong-khanh.vu@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0303 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's yuan fell Thursday morning after hitting six-month highs in two sessions in a row, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the fixing at a slightly weaker level. The yuan is at 6.1316 to a dollar, compared with 6.1299 at Wednesday's close. The central parity is set at 6.1453 versus 6.1425 Wednesday. "After guiding the yuan stronger for two days, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking a break. As it always said, it wants two-way volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY non-deliverable forwards are up at 6.2230/6.2260 from 6.2160/6.2210 late Wednesday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0301 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/TWD is little changed in the absence of fresh trading cues, says a local trader. The trader says foreign fund inflows pare the pair's gains above 29.980, and tips the pair to consolidate between 29.960 and 29.990 for the session. The pair is at 29.979, little changed from 29.970 traded before the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday, which lifted it to 30.025 at the close of onshore trading session, traders say. The Taiex edges down 0.1% after ending 0.8% lower Wednesday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0256 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Look to South Korea's government to attempt to kickstart growth, not \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2 , ahead of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy committee meeting Friday. "The BoK MPC meets tomorrow with a unanimous analyst consensus forecasting an on-hold outcome," \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. "We're surprised by the unanimity of analysts in the face of [finance minister] Choi maintaining the pressure for policy accommodation," the bank adds. "Yesterday \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yonhap\f28 \uc2 \f2  reported him as saying that the 2Q GDP figures showed that the economic recovery was 'extremely weak' and that monetary and fiscal policies should remain supportive of growth." \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut interest rates by 25bps to 2.25% last month, when the Korean government launched a KRW41 trillion stimulus plan. Economic indicators have started improving - \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's South Korea manufacturing PMI flipped from contraction to expansion in August. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The rate of gain in USD/JPY has been too rapid, making the pair vulnerable to a decline, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Kengo Suzuki. The pair has gained about 5% since Aug. 11, when it was at 102.2, or what was the starting point for the recent rally. Calling the USD/JPY rally "one-sided and overheated" in recent sessions, "we can expect an adjustment waiting to happen", says Suzuki. "I get an impression the market is trying to test how far [the USD/JPY] can reach." Now the market participants are closely monitoring what will come out of the meeting between Prime Minister Shinzo Abe and Bank of Japan Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  at 0300 GMT. The USD/JPY is now at 106.78. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The upcoming Shanghai-Hong Kong Stock Connect program, which is expected to launch in October, should give a significant boost to trading volume in China's domestic stock market, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa\f28 \uc2 \f2  research. The house says that foreign money managers will "crowd" into the A-share market as soon as the scheme starts, which could help boost average daily trading volume to 200 billion yuan and 250 billion yuan in the coming years--a higher range than the 150 billion yuan to 220 billion yuan range seen over the last five years. (daniel.inman@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0251 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China releases auto sales data for August this afternoon and analysts are expecting another soft month. The summer is traditionally a period of slow sales but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' Yang Song says other factors are also at play. Based on preliminary market data for the month, she said 1.47 million passenger vehicles were sold in China last month, up 8.6% year on year, down from 9.7% in July. In a research note Wednesday, she also cited de-stocking of inventory by car dealers, a reduction in speculative car buying in anticipation of government car-purchase restrictions, as well as a greater desire by consumers to wait and see if China's antitrust investigations into car makers could trigger price cuts. Those probes seem to have had little influence on the sales performance of foreign automakers, who continue to outperform local car manufacturers, she notes.(colum.murphy@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0246 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's military leaders described cleaning up corruption as one prime reason for seizing power last May. Now they may have a graft case of their own. The Bangkok Post and other media are reporting that the country's auditor-general is investigating the purchase of an expensive audio-conferencing kit for Government House under the military's watch. Investigators want to know why microphone units were ordered at $4,500 each--three times the retail price--and flat-screen TVs at $9,600 when newer models were available at $2,500 apiece. The investigation could help send the message that Prime Minister and army chief Prayuth Chan-ocha is serious about rooting out graft. But as Bangkok risk consultancy PQA Associates notes, "the incident is an embarrassment to the military regime, which has repeatedly stated that removal of corrupt politicians is a cornerstone goal of the coup-installed government." (james.hookway@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0230 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Gold was trading near a three-month low in early Asian trading hours with little support to the precious metal. Investors in Asia are eyeing further price falls and buying gold for festivals and weddings is likely to strengthen only from the end of this month, traders say. Safe haven demand for gold has declined with Ukraine saying Russia has withdrawn more than two-thirds of its troops from the country, an ANZ report says. Spot gold was trading at $1,248.60 an ounce, marginally down from the opening price of $1,249 an ounce at 0210 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0228 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China shares are higher after the release of below-view inflation reading for August as the market may have largely priced in a softening of economy, says Tang Yonggang, chief investment adviser at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Yuan Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.3% at 2326.05, and is tipped to find immediate resistance at 2350. Investors have fully expected a slowing economy, especially after Chinese Premier \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li Keqiang\f28 \uc2 \f2  released a weaker-than-expected money supply reading, says Tang. Reform hopes will continue to push stocks up as Premier Li pledged to carry out reforms in key areas of systemic importance. He favors growth stocks and beneficiaries of mixed-ownership reform. Defense stocks are active; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Avic Aircraft\f28 \uc2 \f2  (000768.SZ) adds 5.4% to CNY16.17. The Shenzhen Composite Index rises 0.6% to 1292.71. (amy.li@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0226 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] AUD/USD outlook remains negative despite surprisingly strong domestic jobs data, with a head & shoulders top pattern targeting 0.8950 while 0.9240 caps, according to ThinkForex. "Whilst the jobs numbers are impressive it should be remembered that this is a volatile series at the best of times," ThinkForex senior market analyst Matt Simpson says. "I expect traders to begin fading any rallies below 0.9240 once the excitement dies down from today's figures." He expects the market to refocus on FOMC staff projections and rate statement next week, where markets are continuing to price in a hawkish outlook for the U.S. "But with high expectations already priced in, we should also be on guard for some greenback losses should the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  fail to deliver, again," he says. AUD/USD last at 0.9183. (david.rogers@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @DavidRogersWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0224 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities hold steady after Chinese inflation data misses estimates and investors weigh stimulus bets, shrugging off fresh US military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the Middle East. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's consumer prices rise by 2.0% year-on-year in August, the weakest rate of increase since April and below expectations of 2.2%. Meanwhile, producer prices fall 1.2% year-on-year, accelerating a decline from a 0.9% decrease reported in July. "It would certainly be a concern that the improving trend in PPI has abated (given inputs such as coal, steel and cement), while \f28 \uc2 \f2 CPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  moves further from target," says Chris Weston, chief market strategist at IG. "It does allow more freedom to explore targeted reflation measures and thus this week's financing numbers are key." The city's benchmark is flat at 24,705.83, while H-shares tumble; the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index falls 0.2% to 11,095.68.(gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 10, 2014 23:03 ET (03:03 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140911ea9b000ak\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwg