\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-China's yuan at highest level since March, focus on August trade data\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2014\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Removes extraneous word before headline)\par     SHANGHAI, Sept 4 (Reuters) - China's yuan edged up against\par the dollar on Thursday, hitting its highest level in nearly six\par months, extending a long rally begun in May.\par     The yuan traded at 6.1340 per dollar on Thursday morning,\par the strongest the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been against the dollar since\par mid-March.\par     The yuan is still 1.55 percent weaker than it was in\par January, before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guided the yuan on a sharp\par depreciation that saw it lose as much as 3.75 percent by late\par April.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate at 6.1666\par per dollar prior to market open, up 0.05 percent from the\par previous fix. The spot rate is currently allowed to trade 2\par percent above or below the midpoint.\par     The spot market opened at 6.1398 per dollar, and was\par changing hands at 6.1355 by midday, 0.09 percent firmer from the\par previous close.\par     The spread between the spot rate and the midpoint been\par widened to 0.50 percent from 0.40 percent at midday on\par Wednesday, a result of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slowly rising while the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has kept the midpoint rate at a low level,\par indicating divergence in views between the market and\par regulators.\par     Trade data due to be released on Sept. 8 was a focus market\par attention, with some traders encouraged to sell dollars,\par although a Reuters poll showed expectations for slower export\par growth in August and a smaller trade surplus than July.\par     "The market expectation for August trade data is quite\par positive, thus companies are confidently selling dollars that\par they hold in hand," said a trader at a foreign bank in Shanghai.\par     A Reuters poll of 23 economists, however, showed exports in\par August likely grew 8.0 percent from a year ago, compared with a\par jump of 14.5 percent in July, while imports were seen growing\par 1.7 percent, after their 1.6 percent drop in July.\par     These projections would produce a monthly trade surplus of\par $40 billion, down from July's $47.3 billion.\par   \par        \par The onshore spot yuan market at a glance: \par  Item                       Current   Previous   Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint              6.1666    6.1697     0.05%\par  Spot yuan                  6.1355    6.1411     0.09%\par  Divergence from midpoint*  -0.50%               \par  Spot change ytd                                 -1.33%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation              34.90%\par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par     \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:\par  Instrument                      Current       Difference\par                                                from\par                                                onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan          *   6.1404        -0.08%\par  Offshore non-deliverable        6.219         -0.84%\par  forwards               **                     \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official 